{"parentUuid":"104c3fa4-6cbf-414d-9e51-c837672b293e","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/brain-swebench/run_cc_one.sh","snippet":"1\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\n2\t# Run ONE pilot task with Claude Code headless (subscription auth, no API key).\n3\t# Usage: run_cc_one.sh <baseline|treated> <instance_id>\n4\t# Produces: results/<arm>/<iid>/{patch.diff, cc_out.json}\n5\tset -uo pipefail\n6\tROOT=/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/brain-swebench\n7\tcd \"$ROOT\"\n8\tarm=\"${1:?arm}\"; iid=\"${2:?instance_id}\"\n9\tMODEL=\"${CC_MODEL:-sonnet}\"\n10\tMAXTURNS=\"${CC_MAX_TURNS:-50}\"\n11\tMODELDIR=\"${MODEL//\\//_}\"\n12\t\n13\t# Treated arm REQUIRES a prebuilt graph. While the graph build is still running, a\n14\t# treated task whose graph isn't ready yet must NOT run graph-less (that silently makes\n15\t# the arm identical to baseline = unfair numbers). Skip WITHOUT writing cc_out.json so\n16\t# run_matrix's idempotency re-runs it on a later pass once the graph exists.\n17\tif [ \"$arm\" = treated ] && [ ! -s \"$ROOT/graphs/$iid/graph.edges.ndjson\" ]; then\n18\t  echo \"[$iid/treated] SKIP: graph not built yet (will retry next pass)\"\n19\t  exit 0\n20\tfi\n21\t\n22\tread -r repo commit < <(.venv/bin/python -c \"\n23\timport json;d={i['instance_id']:i for i in json.load(open(__import__('os').environ.get('TASKS_FILE','pilot_tasks.json')))['instances']}\n24\tr=d['$iid'];print(r['repo'],r['base_commit'])\")\n25\tcache=\"$ROOT/repo-cache/$(echo \"$repo\" | tr '/' '__')\"\n26\twt=\"$ROOT/checkouts/$MODELDIR/$arm/$iid\"\n27\tout=\"$ROOT/results/$MODELDIR/$arm/$iid\"; mkdir -p \"$out\"\n28\t\n29\t# serialize git-worktree ops on the SAME cache repo (portable mkdir lock; concurrency-safe)\n30\tmkdir -p \"$ROOT/.locks\"\n31\tlockdir=\"$ROOT/.locks/$(basename \"$cache\").wt.lock\"\n32\t_lock(){ local t=0; until mkdir \"$lockdir\" 2>/dev/null; do sleep 0.2; t=$((t+1)); [ $t -gt 600 ] && break; done; }\n33\t_unlock(){ rmdir \"$lockdir\" 2>/dev/null || true; }\n34\t\n35\t# fresh detached worktree at base_commit\n36\t_lock\n37\tgit -C \"$cache\" worktree remove --force \"$wt\" 2>/dev/null || true\n38\trm -rf \"$wt\"; mkdir -p \"$(dirname \"$wt\")\"\n39\tif ! git -C \"$cache\" worktree add --force --detach \"$wt\" \"$commit\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n40\t  _unlock; echo \"[$iid] worktree add failed\"; exit 1\n41\tfi\n42\t_unlock\n43\t\n44\t# problem statement -> temp file (avoid arg-length / quoting issues)\n45\tps_file=\"$out/problem.txt\"\n46\t.venv/bin/python -c \"\n47\timport json;d={i['instance_id']:i for i in json.load(open(__import__('os').environ.get('TASKS_FILE','pilot_tasks.json')))['instances']}\n48\topen('$ps_file','w').write(d['$iid']['problem_statement'])\"\n49\t\n50\t# graph tool (treated only): per-instance wrapper with env baked in\n51\tGQ_NOTE=\"\"\n52\tif [ \"$arm\" = treated ]; then\n53\t  gq=\"$ROOT/graphs/$iid/gq\"\n54\t  : > \"$out/gq_calls.log\"   # reset per run so the budget counts THIS run only\n55\t  cat > \"$gq\" <<EOF\n56\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\n57\t# log each invocation so we can measure graph usage per task\n58\techo \"\\$@\" >> \"$out/gq_calls.log\"\n59\t# Hard per-task query budget. haiku sometimes rabbit-holes (40+ queries -> tokens blow up 2x on a\n60\t# minority of tasks, dragging the whole-pilot mean). 20 is far more than a real fix needs (the\n61\t# efficient tasks use 4-6); past it, refuse and tell the agent to ACT. This bounds the pathological\n62\t# runs (the variance driver) WITHOUT touching the graph itself.\n63\tif [ \"\\$(wc -l < \"$out/gq_calls.log\")\" -gt 20 ]; then\n64\t  echo \"graph-query budget reached (20 queries). You already have enough structural context — STOP querying and make the edit now.\"\n65\t  exit 0\n66\tfi\n67\tGRAPH_REPO=\"$wt\" GRAPH_SYMBOLS=\"$ROOT/graphs/$iid/graph.symbols.ndjson\" GRAPH_EDGES=\"$ROOT/graphs/$iid/graph.edges.ndjson\" exec python3 \"$ROOT/graph-query\" \"\\$@\"\n68\tEOF\n69\t  chmod +x \"$gq\"\n70\t  GQ_NOTE=\"\n71\tA precomputed code-graph tool is at: $gq — it answers structural questions in ONE call and\n72\treturns a symbol's REAL SOURCE, so you never open/read a file just to see code.\n73\t  $gq def <name>       the symbol's full source (use INSTEAD of reading the file)\n74\t  $gq callers <name>   who calls it   |   $gq callees <name>  what it calls\n75\t  $gq symbols <substr> find a symbol by name   |   $gq file <path>  a file's outline\n76\t  $gq tests <name>     the tests that cover <name>  |  $gq impls <name>  implementations/overrides\n77\t                       (use these two ONLY when a fix is genuinely cross-cutting — measured to cost\n78\t                        more tokens than they save on typical single-symbol fixes, so not routine)\n79\tBE EFFICIENT — token economy is the entire point of this tool:\n80\t- A typical fix takes 2-5 gq calls TOTAL: one 'symbols' to locate the target, one 'def' to read\n81\t  it (+ maybe 'callers' for blast radius), then EDIT. Stop exploring once you can make the fix.\n82\t- 'def' output IS the complete source. Do NOT Read/cat that file afterward — you already have it.\n83\t- NEVER repeat a query you already ran; the graph is static, the answer will not change.\n84\t- Prefer 'def <exact-name>' over broad 'symbols' sweeps. Narrow the substring; do not re-search.\n85\tUse the graph to LOCATE + READ, then go straight to the edit — do not grep/cat/read files the\n86\tgraph already answered for you. Before editing a function/type, a single '$gq callers <name>' shows\n87\tthe call sites to keep working; handle them so the fix is complete, then edit once.\"\n88\t\n89\t  # Task-scoped brief injected up front (gated on BRIEF_INJECT — a v1 string-match brief measured\n90\t  # net-negative because noisy ranking made the agent distrust + explore). v2 ranks by file:line\n91\t  # pin-points + code-fence APIs + dep-tree exclusion for PRECISE seeds (druid-quality on more tasks).\n92\t  # OFF by default; set BRIEF_INJECT=1 to measure. Generated by the harness, not counted vs the budget.\n93\t  if [ -n \"${BRIEF_INJECT:-}\" ]; then\n94\t    BRIEF=$(GRAPH_REPO=\"$wt\" GRAPH_SYMBOLS=\"$ROOT/graphs/$iid/graph.symbols.ndjson\" \\\n95\t            GRAPH_EDGES=\"$ROOT/graphs/$iid/graph.edges.ndjson\" \\\n96\t            python3 \"$ROOT/graph-query\" brief \"$ps_file\" 2>/dev/null | head -c 8000)\n97\t    if [ -n \"$BRIEF\" ]; then\n98\t      GQ_NOTE=\"$GQ_NOTE\n99\t\n100\tThe graph PRE-SELECTED the code most relevant to this issue (below), ranked by the issue's own\n101\tfile:line and code references. This is almost certainly where the fix goes — edit HERE. Do NOT cat\n102\twhole files or re-search for what the brief already shows; only query the graph if the brief misses it.\n103\t--- GRAPH BRIEF (issue-relevant code, source inline) ---\n104\t$BRIEF\n105\t--- END BRIEF ---\"\n106\t    fi\n107\t  fi\n108\tfi\n109\t\n110\t# CC_NAIVE: run as a NAIVE bash-only agent (no Read/Grep/Glob) — the published-comparable baseline\n111\t# (= mini-swe-agent), where the graph's value is not understated by Claude Code's smart navigation.\n112\t# Applied to BOTH arms; treated still gets the graph. This is the honest measurement the harness\n113\t# docs call for (the strong subscription baseline understates the graph).\n114\tNAIVE_NOTE=\"\"\n115\tif [ -n \"${CC_NAIVE:-}\" ]; then\n116\t  NAIVE_NOTE=\"\n117\tYou do NOT have the Read, Grep, or Glob tools. To read code you MUST 'cat <file>' the ENTIRE file\n118\tvia Bash — you cannot read partially, always cat the whole file to understand it. Search with\n119\t'grep -rn <pattern> <dir>', list with 'find'/'ls'. Read every file you need IN FULL before fixing.\"\n120\tfi\n121\t\n122\t# VERIFY: drive test-driven iteration. The measured failure mode is NEAR-MISSES — haiku navigates to\n123\t# the right code (28/30 same file as gold) and reasons a close fix, then fails on a DETAIL: a missed\n124\t# symmetric location, an edge case (trailing slash), the wrong-but-similar method. One-shot \"just\n125\t# stop\" locks those in. Reproduce -> fix -> run tests -> refine catches the detail; the graph finds\n126\t# the tests fast so iteration is cheap. Gated on VERIFY so it can be measured against the one-shot run.\n127\tVERIFY_NOTE=\"\"\n128\tif [ -n \"${VERIFY:-}\" ]; then\n129\t  VERIFY_NOTE=\"\n130\tVERIFY BY RUNNING TESTS — do NOT stop after one edit. This bug is subtle: a fix that is CLOSE but\n131\twrong on a detail (a missed second location, an edge case, the wrong-but-similar method) still fails.\n132\t1. Reproduce FIRST: from the issue's example(s), write a tiny script that exercises the bug and run\n133\t   it — confirm it fails as the issue describes. (This is your scratch check, not a repo test file.)\n\n... [83 lines truncated] ..."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"d76d5652-aa49-45bd-88ee-5acfbd21a126","timestamp":"2026-07-19T13:46:02.254Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.201","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
