{"parentUuid":"fb00f432-6301-427a-9529-1c89654a52f5","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Ce8T874HN2Ay9gorPjVTp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JhPgeBWoFVre41e8mTdomh","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"gh pr view 85 --repo entireio/entire-brain --json comments --jq '.comments[0].body' 2>/dev/null | sed -n '10,90p'","description":"Read full review of PR 85"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1423,"cache_read_input_tokens":575820,"output_tokens":333,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":136},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1423,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":333,"cache_read_input_tokens":575820,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1423,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1423},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Ce8T82gc8PtV94pzhqRmr","type":"assistant","uuid":"b3307be7-1db4-495b-a030-ec69b349afc8","timestamp":"2026-08-17T14:07:48.371Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"f507d58c-285f-4d11-b1d0-201f2a5e172d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/p0p1/p1-new","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.233","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
{"parentUuid":"b3307be7-1db4-495b-a030-ec69b349afc8","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"9b632cc4-8ae4-4a21-90b3-b370830204c2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JhPgeBWoFVre41e8mTdomh","type":"tool_result","content":"**Canonicalization (`c64327e4`, `2d8dd890`):**\n- `Canonicalize` now rejects **duplicate JSON members** via a token-level decoder. Go's last-wins decode meant a signature verified over re-canonicalized bytes while a first-wins parser in another language read the first-occurrence values from the same \"verified\" byte stream.\n- That decoder is **depth-bounded (512 levels)** — unbounded recursion made a few MB of `[` a fatal stack overflow in the entry point for untrusted bytes.\n- **Lowercase `t`/`z`** timestamp separators (RFC 3339 §5.6-legal) now parse and normalize.\n- **Integers bounded to ±2^53**: beyond that a double-based JCS verifier re-serializes a different value and signatures silently fail cross-language. The integers-only profile is now documented as deliberate — a future minor needing non-integer numbers must ship a new `Signature.CanonicalEncoding` identifier rather than widen the rule.\n\n**Proposal queue (`c64327e4`, `2d8dd890`):**\n- `ResolveOpen` pins the proposal ID after the first ref resolution; a CAS retry can no longer rebind a candidate-id or prefix ref to a different proposal that entered the set mid-retry.\n- Reject is now the prune path for proposals whose facts left the head (previously nothing ever removed them); Accept still fails loudly.\n- Prefix refs count only characters past the constant `prop-` — the ref `prop` used to match every open proposal.\n- `facts sync` fails on real publish errors instead of warning (the facts had already advanced the head, and the identical-content short-circuit made a swallowed failure permanent); only a genuinely queue-less backend — 404 on the collection *List* — downgrades to a warning.\n- A per-branch shared-proposal ledger reconciles the local queue against the hosted set: never-shared entries publish on the next sync, teammate-settled entries are pruned rather than resurrected. Publishing requires `ProposedBy`, which keeps distill's private single-user review backlog off the wire.\n- Doc correction: two members raising the same conflict produce *distinct* IDs (`ProposedBy` is part of the derivation); republish idempotence holds per member.\n\n### Open design questions — flagged, not patched\n\nThese reshape the model rather than repair it, so they are left for a human decision:\n\n1. **Accepted settlements don't stick.** The losing member's local facts are never reconciled to a hosted resolution; their next sync's keep-both `Promote` re-adds the retired fact and re-raises the conflict under a fresh ID. Settlements likely need to be pulled down into local facts during sync.\n2. **Local `facts review` has no hosted interlock.** Settling a published proposal locally leaves the hosted copy open; a teammate's later apply silently overrides the member's decision.\n3. **Hosted settlement leaves no tombstone.** The anti-resurrection ledger is per-checkout, so a second clone of the same member republishes settled conflicts. A server-side settled-ID record would close this for all clients.\n4. **`distill --force` rewrites `proposals.ndjson` without the prior backlog**, silently deleting sync-raised conflicts nobody reviewed. The cross-member queue probably should not share a file distill owns.\n5. **Orphaned proposals with a missing facts head are unremovable**: the empty-head guard fires before the reject-prune path, and the client refuses to push an empty head. Needs a prune-only server operation.\n\nItems 1 and 2 look worth settling before merge.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"58bef2c6-e737-45f1-9f3e-29b27ef07b92","timestamp":"2026-08-17T14:07:49.207Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"**Canonicalization (`c64327e4`, `2d8dd890`):**\n- `Canonicalize` now rejects **duplicate JSON members** via a token-level decoder. Go's last-wins decode meant a signature verified over re-canonicalized bytes while a first-wins parser in another language read the first-occurrence values from the same \"verified\" byte stream.\n- That decoder is **depth-bounded (512 levels)** — unbounded recursion made a few MB of `[` a fatal stack overflow in the entry point for untrusted bytes.\n- **Lowercase `t`/`z`** timestamp separators (RFC 3339 §5.6-legal) now parse and normalize.\n- **Integers bounded to ±2^53**: beyond that a double-based JCS verifier re-serializes a different value and signatures silently fail cross-language. The integers-only profile is now documented as deliberate — a future minor needing non-integer numbers must ship a new `Signature.CanonicalEncoding` identifier rather than widen the rule.\n\n**Proposal queue (`c64327e4`, `2d8dd890`):**\n- `ResolveOpen` pins the proposal ID after the first ref resolution; a CAS retry can no longer rebind a candidate-id or prefix ref to a different proposal that entered the set mid-retry.\n- Reject is now the prune path for proposals whose facts left the head (previously nothing ever removed them); Accept still fails loudly.\n- Prefix refs count only characters past the constant `prop-` — the ref `prop` used to match every open proposal.\n- `facts sync` fails on real publish errors instead of warning (the facts had already advanced the head, and the identical-content short-circuit made a swallowed failure permanent); only a genuinely queue-less backend — 404 on the collection *List* — downgrades to a warning.\n- A per-branch shared-proposal ledger reconciles the local queue against the hosted set: never-shared entries publish on the next sync, teammate-settled entries are pruned rather than resurrected. Publishing requires `ProposedBy`, which keeps distill's private single-user review backlog off the wire.\n- Doc correction: two members raising the same conflict produce *distinct* IDs (`ProposedBy` is part of the derivation); republish idempotence holds per member.\n\n### Open design questions — flagged, not patched\n\nThese reshape the model rather than repair it, so they are left for a human decision:\n\n1. **Accepted settlements don't stick.** The losing member's local facts are never reconciled to a hosted resolution; their next sync's keep-both `Promote` re-adds the retired fact and re-raises the conflict under a fresh ID. Settlements likely need to be pulled down into local facts during sync.\n2. **Local `facts review` has no hosted interlock.** Settling a published proposal locally leaves the hosted copy open; a teammate's later apply silently overrides the member's decision.\n3. **Hosted settlement leaves no tombstone.** The anti-resurrection ledger is per-checkout, so a second clone of the same member republishes settled conflicts. A server-side settled-ID record would close this for all clients.\n4. **`distill --force` rewrites `proposals.ndjson` without the prior backlog**, silently deleting sync-raised conflicts nobody reviewed. The cross-member queue probably should not share a file distill owns.\n5. **Orphaned proposals with a missing facts head are unremovable**: the empty-head guard fires before the reject-prune path, and the client refuses to push an empty head. Needs a prune-only server operation.\n\nItems 1 and 2 look worth settling before merge.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b3307be7-1db4-495b-a030-ec69b349afc8","session_id":"f507d58c-285f-4d11-b1d0-201f2a5e172d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/p0p1/p1-new","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.233","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
