{"parentUuid":"ddd6f7c5-0e61-465f-b02c-9e7af4a84e08","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14911845 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"1b5eb0e7-8f04-449f-8d64-f313bb02ab5f","timestamp":"2026-08-17T15:36:41.856Z","session_id":"f507d58c-285f-4d11-b1d0-201f2a5e172d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/p0p1/p1-new","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.233","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
{"parentUuid":"1b5eb0e7-8f04-449f-8d64-f313bb02ab5f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Ce8Zuvrbb2tVesu4GfAe4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Final verification running clean (nothing competing this time). Watcher `byxxpv0f3` reports, then I push everything to #85.\n\nAdded since the last update — and this one I'd flag as the most interesting find:\n\n**A flaky test that failed my own verification, root-caused rather than retried away.** `TestBrainBriefGitIntentLiveShapedOperationalRecall` drives **real git** through `ExecRunner`, and the overlay is deliberately bounded at 500ms so a slow repo can't stall a brief. Under load the two git calls miss that window, the overlay correctly degrades to baseline, and the test that *requires* activation fails — a good implementation failing for environmental reasons. Fixed with a bounded 5-attempt retry, and **verified it still catches real breakage** by stubbing the promoter to always return false (it fails, as it must).\n\nSecond, smaller one in the same file: the hard-timeout case budgeted `timeout + 500ms` = 1s total. Since the runner blocks on `ctx.Done()`, a broken bound doesn't return *late* — it never returns at all. So the slack only needs to exclude that, not measure scheduler wake-up.\n\nWorth naming explicitly: **that 500ms bound also means the git-intent overlay silently stops contributing to retrieval on a loaded machine in production**, not just in tests. The code comment acknowledges the tradeoff (\"without turning scheduler delay into a random retrieval miss\"), so it's intentional — but it is a real accuracy-under-load characteristic, and I've left the product behavior alone rather than trading latency for it unasked."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":300,"cache_read_input_tokens":781180,"output_tokens":530,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens"