{"parentUuid":"e5afc9ce-aec5-494d-a7be-f94878163a76","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Ce8awuSBoqYDTRGD7JksP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2307,"cache_read_input_tokens":801495,"output_tokens":1870,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":525},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2307,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1870,"cache_read_input_tokens":801495,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2307,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2307},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Ce8awoJbEXNambiYf95B4","type":"assistant","uuid":"6c749f2c-9323-4c46-aa3b-6bd1c3e8da81","timestamp":"2026-08-17T15:50:27.439Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"f507d58c-285f-4d11-b1d0-201f2a5e172d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/p0p1/p1-new","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.233","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
{"parentUuid":"6c749f2c-9323-4c46-aa3b-6bd1c3e8da81","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Ce8awuSBoqYDTRGD7JksP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That test settles it — and my change was **wrong**. Read the second half: *\"an older reader must not be able to strip fields it does not understand and still validate.\"*\n\nThe signature covers the unknown fields, so adding *or* removing them invalidates it. That closes the smuggling concern I raised, and preserving them is what makes additive-only 1.x evolution work. **Reverting my change** — the defect was in my test's assumption, not the code:"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2307,"cache_read_input_tokens":801495,"output_tokens":1870,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":525},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2307,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1870,"cache_read_input_tokens":801495,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2307,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2307},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Ce8awoJbEXNambiYf95B4","type":"assistant","uuid":"49e2f29c-f092-4664-bd05-4afa62a9a939","timestamp":"2026-08-17T15:50:29.401Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"f507d58c-285f-4d11-b1d0-201f2a5e172d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/p0p1/p1-new","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.233","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
{"parentUuid":"49e2f29c-f092-4664-bd05-4afa62a9a939","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Ce8awuSBoqYDTRGD7JksP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LiDu4QYNDUcuPgSzDYtMCd","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/p0p1/p1-new/internal/brainwire/canonical.go","old_string":"\t// Unknown fields are REJECTED, not passed through. Canonical form exists only to\n\t// be signed and verified, and a field the contract cannot represent must never sit\n\t// inside signed bytes:\n\t//\n\t//   - It breaks the fixed point. Canonicalize keeps such a field, while the struct\n\t//     path (CanonicalUnmarshal then CanonicalMarshal) drops it, so the two produce\n\t//     different bytes for one logical artifact and a signature made through one\n\t//     will not verify through the other.\n\t//   - It allows field smuggling. A producer attaches extra keys, the signature\n\t//     covers them, and a verifier that decodes into BrainArtifact never sees them\n\t//     yet still validates — two implementations disagreeing about the content while\n\t//     both consider the signature good. That is the same confusion class as the\n\t//     duplicate-member rejection above, reached by a different route.\n\t//\n\t// Forward compatibility for READING newer minors is a separate concern, served by\n\t// the tolerant decode path and CheckCompatibility; canonicalization is not that\n\t// path. Per ADR 0001 the 1.x major is additive-only, and a minor that adds a field\n\t// must ship a new Signature.CanonicalEncoding identifier — the same rule the\n\t// integer bound already follows — rather than have old verifiers silently sign\n\t// bytes they cannot interpret.\n\tif len(in) != len(known) {\n\t\tunknown := make([]string, 0, len(in))\n\t\tfor k := range in {\n\t\t\tif _, ok := known[k]; !ok {\n\t\t\t\tunknown = append(unknown, k)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tif len(unknown) > 0 {\n\t\t\tsort.Strings(unknown)\n\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"brainwire: %s: unknown field(s) %v are not part of the canonical encoding; a newer minor must ship a new canonical-encoding identifier\", t.Name, unknown)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn out, nil\n}","new_string":"\t// Unknown (newer-minor) fields pass through; only their encoding is\n\t// canonicalized, by encodeValue. This is deliberate and load-bearing for the\n\t// additive-only 1.x contract in ADR 0001: an older verifier must be able to\n\t// canonicalize and verify a NEWER producer's artifact, which it cannot do if an\n\t// unrecognized key is fatal.\n\t//\n\t// It does not permit field smuggling, because the signature is taken over these\n\t// bytes INCLUDING the unknown fields: adding one changes the signed input, and\n\t// stripping one that a reader does not understand also invalidates the signature\n\t// (TestVerifyBytesToleratesUnknownFields pins both directions).\n\t//\n\t// The consequence to know: the struct path cannot represent these fields, so\n\t// CanonicalMarshal(CanonicalUnmarshal(b)) == b holds only for bytes whose keys are\n\t// all known. The fixed point that matters for signing — Canonicalize being\n\t// idempotent — holds either way; see the fuzz tests.\n\tfor k, v := range in {\n\t\tif _, ok := known[k]; ok {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tout[k] = v\n\t}\n\treturn out, nil\n}"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2307,"cache_read_input_tokens":801495,"output_tokens":1870,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":525},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2307,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1870,"cache_read_input_tokens":801495,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2307,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemer