{"parentUuid":"0639f31f-eb64-4583-9985-bbd150697b4e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"df02332c-6f37-42f9-9a7b-d8df51e24aec","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LiDu4QYNDUcuPgSzDYtMCd","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/suhaan/devenv/p0p1/p1-new/internal/brainwire/canonical.go has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"69c40f55-c48f-42e7-a058-75448f587a51","timestamp":"2026-08-17T15:50:40.816Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/p0p1/p1-new/internal/brainwire/canonical.go","oldString":"\t// Unknown fields are REJECTED, not passed through. Canonical form exists only to\n\t// be signed and verified, and a field the contract cannot represent must never sit\n\t// inside signed bytes:\n\t//\n\t//   - It breaks the fixed point. Canonicalize keeps such a field, while the struct\n\t//     path (CanonicalUnmarshal then CanonicalMarshal) drops it, so the two produce\n\t//     different bytes for one logical artifact and a signature made through one\n\t//     will not verify through the other.\n\t//   - It allows field smuggling. A producer attaches extra keys, the signature\n\t//     covers them, and a verifier that decodes into BrainArtifact never sees them\n\t//     yet still validates — two implementations disagreeing about the content while\n\t//     both consider the signature good. That is the same confusion class as the\n\t//     duplicate-member rejection above, reached by a different route.\n\t//\n\t// Forward compatibility for READING newer minors is a separate concern, served by\n\t// the tolerant decode path and CheckCompatibility; canonicalization is not that\n\t// path. Per ADR 0001 the 1.x major is additive-only, and a minor that adds a field\n\t// must ship a new Signature.CanonicalEncoding identifier — the same rule the\n\t// integer bound already follows — rather than have old verifiers silently sign\n\t// bytes they cannot interpret.\n\tif len(in) != len(known) {\n\t\tunknown := make([]string, 0, len(in))\n\t\tfor k := range in {\n\t\t\tif _, ok := known[k]; !ok {\n\t\t\t\tunknown = append(unknown, k)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tif len(unknown) > 0 {\n\t\t\tsort.Strings(unknown)\n\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"brainwire: %s: unknown field(s) %v are not part of the canonical encoding; a newer minor must ship a new canonical-encoding identifier\", t.Name, unknown)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn out, nil\n}","newString":"\t// Unknown (newer-minor) fields pass through; only their encoding is\n\t// canonicalized, by encodeValue. This is deliberate and load-bearing for the\n\t// additive-only 1.x contract in ADR 0001: an older verifier must be able to\n\t// canonicalize and verify a NEWER producer's artifact, which it cannot do if an\n\t// unrecognized key is fatal.\n\t//\n\t// It does not permit field smuggling, because the signature is taken over these\n\t// bytes INCLUDING the unknown fields: adding one changes the signed input, and\n\t// stripping one that a reader does not understand also invalidates the signature\n\t// (TestVerifyBytesToleratesUnknownFields pins both directions).\n\t//\n\t// The consequence to know: the struct path cannot represent these fields, so\n\t// CanonicalMarshal(CanonicalUnmarshal(b)) == b holds only for bytes whose keys are\n\t// all known. The fixed point that matters for signing — Canonicalize being\n\t// idempotent — holds either way; see the fuzz tests.\n\tfor k, v := range in {\n\t\tif _, ok := known[k]; ok {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tout[k] = v\n\t}\n\treturn out, nil\n}","originalFile":null,"structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":337,"oldLines":37,"newStart":337,"newLines":26,"lines":["       out[f.Key] = v","     }","   }","-  // Unknown fields are REJECTED, not passed through. Canonical form exists only to","-  // be signed and verified, and a field the contract cannot represent must never sit","-  // inside signed bytes:","+  // Unknown (newer-minor) fields pass through; only 