{"parentUuid":"895e3a4c-17dd-47b1-99a0-c5136b0f104b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Ce8jSEh2ftvuGUagJDaCw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01U1HxPrfPqrHWbaagqmz9VS","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/suhaan/devenv/p0p1/p1-new\ngofmt -s -l internal/ | head -3\ngo build ./... 2>&1|head -2\ngit add internal/factsync/httpproposals.go\ngit commit -q -F - <<'EOF'\nfix(factsync): read 503 from the proposal queue as \"no queue here\"\n\nentire-api answers 503 whenever the hosted brain is disabled — the same way its\nfact-set endpoints do — but the client only treated 404 and 501 as an absent queue, so\na deployment with the brain switched off surfaced as an unexpected-status error\ninstead of degrading. That failed a sync whose facts had already landed.\n\nRead it as unsupported on both the list and publish paths, so the proposals stay in\nthe local review queue with a warning. A genuinely transient 503 costs one cycle: the\nnext sync re-checks and publishes then.\n\nFound by diffing this client against the server it talks to rather than by running it;\nthe two live in different repos, so no test on either side covered the boundary.\nEOF\ngit push entireio HEAD:feat/p1-canonical-signing-proposals --quiet 2>&1 | tail -1\ngit log --oneline -1","description":"Commit and push the 503 client fix"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1387,"cache_read_input_tokens":901152,"output_tokens":471,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1387,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":471,"cache_read_input_tokens":901152,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1387,"cache_creation":{"e