{"parentUuid":"2732c551-345d-40a1-9ca0-68102f9d8245","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeAPfECJkCS1eZWAVrsEy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0168yohP6KQCQgTQfbY26Gks","name":"mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__search_files","input":{"query":"fullText contains 'P2' and (fullText contains 'brain' or fullText contains 'entire')","pageSize":15},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2560,"cache_read_input_tokens":932481,"output_tokens":120,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2560,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":120,"cache_read_input_tokens":932481,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2560,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2560},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CeAPf6ceSVQVKrSGdMgYb","type":"assistant","uuid":"36c85f1f-c59c-4f7a-8d42-aa60376bc9c2","timestamp":"2026-08-18T14:43:30.232Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"f507d58c-285f-4d11-b1d0-201f2a5e172d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/p0p1/api-242","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.233","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
{"parentUuid":"36c85f1f-c59c-4f7a-8d42-aa60376bc9c2","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"697b2785-9eb9-4fad-a497-7816c3eae47a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0168yohP6KQCQgTQfbY26Gks","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"files\":[{\"canAddChildren\":false,\"contentSnippet\":\"# ð§  The Semantic Reasoning Layer: Roadmap\\n\\nTurn the local brain into a shared reasoning substrate that a fleet of agents builds on. Git preserves change, but it does not preserve meaning. This initiative gives every agent persistent, shared, pluggable memory tied to git history, so humans and agents can reason and build in parallel without colliding.\\n\\nInitiative: [Semantic Reasoning Layer](https://linear.app/entirehq/initiative/semantic-reasoning-layer-e043385c2167) (Linear, Active) · Vision: [entire.io/vision](https://entire.io/vision) · As of: July 2026\\n\\nThe arc: ð¢ P0 finish the brain, then ðµ P1 host it, then ð£ P2 unify memory behind one surface, then ð  P3 coordinate fleets and ð´ P4 federate across repos in parallel, all feeding ⭐ P5, the north star: behavioral intelligence, where retained behavior becomes decisions (best-agent routing, prompt quality per person, a best-prompt library).\\n\\nGoverning constraint: no new infrastructure. The whole arc rides entire.io's existing rails (entiredb, checkpoint\\\\_remote, snapshotstore and snapshotcrypt, authz and SpiceDB, the jurisdiction forwarder). Each milestone ports proven parts onto the brain model rather than building storage or crypto from scratch.\\n\\n## ð Timeline at a glance\\n\\nTarget: the whole roadmap complete by end of March 2027. The schedule below is the compressed plan to hit that date. The dates run tight and in parallel from P2 onward.\\n\\n| Phase | Focus | Window | Status |\\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\\n| ð¢ P0 GA the local brain | Harden entire-sem, brain, MCP surface, bench | Jul to Aug 2026 | Ships today, GA wrapper in progress |\\n| ðµ P1 Shared / hosted brain | Server store, sync, remote query, auth | Now, ship Aug 2026 | Built and tested on clones, one step from prod |\\n| ð§© Keystone: semantic index tied to VCS | Entity deltas, git-native index, causality | Aug to Sep 2026 | Planned |\\n| ð£ P2 Unified memory, one MCP surface | Converge search, one backbone, one surface | Sep to Nov 2026 | Planned |\\n| ð¡ Reasoning capture, compounding memory | Record schema, write-back, provenance graph | Oct to Dec 2026 | Planned |\\n| ð  P3 Coordinated agents | Claims, conflict detection, handovers, scheduler | Nov 2026 to Feb 2027 | Planned |\\n| ð´ P4 Cross-repo federation | Federated discovery, capability registry | Jan to Mar 2027 | Planned |\\n| ⭐ P5 Behavioral intelligence (north star) | Prompt quality, best-prompt library, fit, auto-routing | Jan to Jun 2027 | Planned |\\n\\nThe March 2027 target covers the enabling phases (P0 through P4). The P5 north star starts as soon as P2's surface and P3's coordination land, and runs to June 2027. Linear milestone dates now match the compressed schedule.\\n\\n## ð¢ P0, GA the local brain\\n\\nWhat it is. The foundation the whole initiative stands on. The local brain already runs, so P0 is finish work and release hygiene, not new build. There is no gate here. Everything ships today, but nothing is formally tagged, so P0 is the wrapper that makes it something the rest of the arc can depend on.\\n\\nThe pieces, all live today.\\n\\nentire-sem is the semantic provider. It parses code into symbols and relations with tree-sitter, and it uses no vectors anywhere in the stack.\\n\\nentire-brain is the local brain. It holds durable, branch-scoped, provenance-anchored facts, plus a local MCP surface of about 33 tools that agents query.\\n\\nbrain-bench is the CI gate. It benchmarks the extractor against a reference tool across roughly 30 languages and fails the build on regression or drift.\\n\\nThe milestones.\\n\\nM1, harden and GA entire-sem. Version-stamp it and freeze the schema so everything downstream can trust the contract.\\n\\nM2, close the brain blockers. Ship the local brain with honest claims, no overreach.\\n\\nM3, GA the MCP surface for both plugins. Harden the local tool surface for the Claude Code and Codex plugins.\\n\\nM4, brain-bench as the automated eval gate. Make the benchmark a required regression floor.\\n\\nM5, release logistics. The release checklist, the compatibility matrix, and distribution.\\n\\nFoundation build tracked in Linear: [Semantic Index Tied to VCS History](https://linear.app/entirehq/project/semantic-index-tied-to-vcs-history-ea453fa387e2) (Urgent). This is the piece that persists entity-level meaning onto checkpoints and a git-native index, so meaning is stored with git history and not re-parsed on demand. Done when entire and MCP can answer \\\"which checkpoints changed entity X\\\" from the index.\\n\\nEntity deltas in checkpoint metadata (target 2026-08-15): [COR-781](https://linear.app/entirehq/issue/COR-781/spike-map-semantic-extractor-snapshot-checkpoint-metadata-schema) spike the schema, then [COR-782](https://linear.app/entirehq/issue/COR-782/embed-entity-deltas-into-the-checkpoint-metadata-write-path) embed the deltas into the write path.\\n\\nGit-native entity to checkpoint index (target 2026-09-15): [COR-783](https://linear.app/entirehq/issue/COR-783/build-the-git-native-entity-checkpoint-index) build the index, [COR-784]...\",\"createdTime\":\"2026-07-09T01:18:49Z\",\"fileExtension\":\"docx\",\"fileSize\":\"517504\",\"id\":\"REDACTED\",\"mimeType\":\"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document\",\"modifiedTime\":\"2026-07-10T02:00:40.110Z\",\"owner\":\"suhaan@entire.io\",\"sharedWithMeTime\":\"2026-07-09T19:56:46.035Z\",\"title\":\"Semantic Reasoning Layer - Roadmap.docx\",\"viewUrl\":\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view?usp=drivesdk\",\"viewedByMeTime\":\"2026-07-09T20:01:19.867Z\"},{\"canAddChildren\":false,\"contentSnippet\":\"Harvard Undergraduate\\n\\nOpen Bio\\n\\nLaboratory\\n\\nPRESENTS\\n\\nSRI\\n\\nSTUDENT RESEARCH INSTITUTE\\n\\nISSUE 2 SUMMER '25\\n\\n2\\n\\nTable of Contents\\n\\nORIGINAL RESEARCH\\n\\nCUPNavigator: A multi-model CDSS for tumor origin prediction, diagnostic planning, and therapy recommendations in cancer of unknown primary Saicharan Vellanki and Salomon Kobongo\\n\\nDROID: Dual-headed Representation for Omics-based Identification of SARS-CoV-2 Infection and Cell type via Deep Learning Enan Khan and Tuan Pham\\n\\nIntegrative lipidomics and network pharmacology reveal the mechanisms of cannabidiol in the treatment of acute myeloid leukemia Aiden Yoon and Tomiwa Oyedokun\\n\\nBi-stable Auxetic Stent Design for Customized Vascular Conformability Tarun Malarvasan and Hrishikesh Kanade\\n\\nDecoding the dark proteome: Deep learning-enabled discovery of druggable enzymes in Wuchereria bancrofti Shawnak Shivakumar and Jefferson Hernandez\\n\\nLITERATURE REVIEW\\n\\nThe potential of CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing technology to enhance CAR T-cell therapy as a targeted treatment for leukemia Adwita Jagannathan and Daniella Lu\\n\\nReassessing the Undruggable: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of KRAS G12C Inhibitor Efficacy in 2,031 Cancer Patients Suchir Ryali and Andrew Evans\\n\\nInterplay of Microglia and the Glymphatic system in Alzheimer’s Disease A Systematic Review on the Crosstalk of Microglia and the Glymphatic System in Alzheimer’s Disease Dhriti Kantipudi and Hannah Yoder\\n\\nThe dialect of chromatin in chronic pain and its distortion under opioid exposure Sahana Sivam and Carlos Rivera\\n\\nThe role of stress-related biomolecules in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma: A tidal wave of consequences\\n\\nVivian Zhao and Brielle Patlin\\n\\nCONFERENCE PROCEEDINGS\\n\\nSTEM Fellowship Journal vol.0 ● issue 0\\n\\n3\\n\\nLetter Letter from from the the Harvard Harvard Undergraduate Undergraduate OpenBio OpenBio Laboratory Laboratory Student Student Research Research Institute Institute Team Team\\n\\nDear Readers,\\n\\nIt is a joy and an honor to share this issue of STEM Fellowship Journal, encapsulating the inquisitiveness, dedication, and commitment to science of the Student Research Institute (SRI) Summer 2025 cohort. Within these pages, you will find the work of students who remind us why science matters and why access to it must be protected and expanded. The student researchers of this second cohort of SRI have shown us that curiosity knows no boundaries of geography, background, or circumstance.\\n\\nSRI was created with the simple belief that every young person with a question burning inside them deserves the chance to explore it. Every student who dreams of research deserves a seat at the table, regardless of whether their school has a lab, their family can pay for a program, or they even know what “research” looks like. This summer, more than 2,000 students from 38 countries reached out with that dream. One hundred and fifteen were selected to embark on an eight-week journey guided by mentors from 32 universities. Together, they explored fields from CRISPR-CAR T-cell therapy to tumor origin prediction. They read, they asked, they struggled, they discovered. And in the end, they produced papers and posters that reflect not only what they learned but who they are becoming as scientists.\\n\\nNone of this would have been possible without the extraordinary mentors who gave their time and care. Balancing their own demanding research, they chose to teach, to guide, and to believe in the potential of students they had never met before. Their generosity created spaces where curiosity could take root and where confidence could begin to grow. We are deeply grateful for their example.\\n\\nThe pages that follow are filled with the beginnings of scientific journeys. Yet they are more than first steps. They are reminders of what science can be when access is widened and voices once overlooked are invited in. They are proof that talent is everywhere, waiting for an open door.\\n\\nAs you read, we invite you not only to celebrate the accomplishments of these young researchers but also to imagine what it means for the future of science when they are given the tools to thrive. The discoveries they make in the years ahead may change fields we cannot yet name. What matters now is that they have begun, and that they know they belong here.\\n\\nOn behalf of the Harvard Undergraduate OpenBio Laboratory, we extend our heartfelt congratulations to this year’s students, our gratitude to their mentors, and our hope for all the journeys that lie ahead. The future of science is undoubtedly brighter because of them.\\n\\nSahil Sood Co-Director of Engagement, SRI Co-Lead , Harvard Undergraduate OpenBio Laboratory\\n\\nAlex Merheb Co-Director of Engagement, SRI Co-Lead, Harvard Undergraduate OpenBio Laboratory\\n\\nSean Meng Co-President, Harvard Undergraduate OpenBio Laboratory\\n\\nHugh Hankenson Co-President, Harvard Undergraduate OpenBio Laboratory\\n\\n2026 2026 journal.stemfellowship.org journal.stemfellowship.org\\n\\n4 ORIGINAL INVESTIGATIONS\\n\\n1ST PLACE, ORIGINAL RESEARC...\",\"createdTime\":\"2026-03-26T20:17:33.898Z\",\"fileExtension\":\"pdf\",\"fileSize\":\"9015122\",\"id\":\"REDACTED\",\"mimeType\":\"application/pdf\",\"modifiedTime\":\"2026-03-26T20:19:07.509Z\",\"owner\":\"ashao@college.harvard.edu\",\"sharedWithMeTime\":\"2026-04-22T21:44:48.287Z\",\"title\":\"SRI_Issue_V3.pdf\",\"viewUrl\":\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view?usp=drivesdk\",\"viewedByMeTime\":\"2026-05-27T01:57:04.734Z\"}]}"}]},"uuid":"d015e302-f155-4e0b-901f-520c61db2c9b","timestamp":"2026-08-18T14:43:31.500Z","toolUseResult":"{\"files\":[{\"canAddChildren\":false,\"contentSnippet\":\"# ð§  The Semantic Reasoning Layer: Roadmap\\n\\nTurn the local brain into a shared reasoning substrate that a fleet of agents builds on. Git preserves change, but it does not preserve meaning. This initiative gives every agent persistent, shared, pluggable memory tied to git history, so humans and agents can reason and build in parallel without colliding.\\n\\nInitiative: [Semantic Reasoning Layer](https://linear.app/entirehq/initiative/semantic-reasoning-layer-e043385c2167) (Linear, Active) · Vision: [entire.io/vision](https://entire.io/vision) · As of: July 2026\\n\\nThe arc: ð¢ P0 finish the brain, then ðµ P1 host it, then ð£ P2 unify memory behind one surface, then ð  P3 coordinate fleets and ð´ P4 federate across repos in parallel, all feeding ⭐ P5, the north star: behavioral intelligence, where retained behavior becomes decisions (best-agent routing, prompt quality per person, a best-prompt library).\\n\\nGoverning constraint: no new infrastructure. The whole arc rides entire.io's existing rails (entiredb, checkpoint\\\\_remote, snapshotstore and snapshotcrypt, authz and SpiceDB, the jurisdiction forwarder). Each milestone ports proven parts onto the brain model rather than building storage or crypto from scratch.\\n\\n## ð Timeline at a glance\\n\\nTarget: the whole roadmap complete by end of March 2027. The schedule below is the compressed plan to hit that date. The dates run tight and in parallel from P2 onward.\\n\\n| Phase | Focus | Window | Status |\\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\\n| ð¢ P0 GA the local brain | Harden entire-sem, brain, MCP surface, bench | Jul to Aug 2026 | Ships today, GA wrapper in progress |\\n| ðµ P1 Shared / hosted brain | Server store, sync, remote query, auth | Now, ship Aug 2026 | Built and tested on clones, one step from prod |\\n| ð§© Keystone: semantic index tied to VCS | Entity deltas, git-native index, causality | Aug to Sep 2026 | Planned |\\n| ð£ P2 Unified memory, one MCP surface | Converge search, one backbone, one surface | Sep to Nov 2026 | Planned |\\n| ð¡ Reasoning capture, compounding memory | Record schema, write-back, provenance graph | Oct to Dec 2026 | Planned |\\n| ð  P3 Coordinated agents | Claims, conflict detection, handovers, scheduler | Nov 2026 to Feb 2027 | Planned |\\n| ð´ P4 Cross-repo federation | Federated discovery, capability registry | Jan to Mar 2027 | Planned |\\n| ⭐ P5 Behavioral intelligence (north star) | Prompt quality, best-prompt library, fit, auto-routing | Jan to Jun 2027 | Planned |\\n\\nThe March 2027 target covers the enabling phases (P0 through P4). The P5 north star starts as soon as P2's surface and P3's coordination land, and runs to June 2027. Linear milestone dates now match the compressed schedule.\\n\\n## ð¢ P0, GA the local brain\\n\\nWhat it is. The foundation the whole initiative stands on. The local brain already runs, so P0 is finish work and release hygiene, not new build. There is no gate here. Everything ships today, but nothing is formally tagged, so P0 is the wrapper that makes it something the rest of the arc can depend on.\\n\\nThe pieces, all live today.\\n\\nentire-sem is the semantic provider. It parses code into symbols and relations with tree-sitter, and it uses no vectors anywhere in the stack.\\n\\nentire-brain is the local brain. It holds durable, branch-scoped, provenance-anchored facts, plus a local MCP surface of about 33 tools that agents query.\\n\\nbrain-bench is the CI gate. It benchmarks the extractor against a reference tool across roughly 30 languages and fails the build on regression or drift.\\n\\nThe milestones.\\n\\nM1, harden and GA entire-sem. Version-stamp it and freeze the schema so everything downstream can trust the contract.\\n\\nM2, close the brain blockers. Ship the local brain with honest claims, no overreach.\\n\\nM3, GA the MCP surface for both plugins. Harden the local tool surface for the Claude Code and Codex plugins.\\n\\nM4, brain-bench as the automated eval gate. Make the benchmark a required regression floor.\\n\\nM5, release logistics. The release checklist, the compatibility matrix, and distribution.\\n\\nFoundation build tracked in Linear: [Semantic Index Tied to VCS History](https://linear.app/entirehq/project/semantic-index-tied-to-vcs-history-ea453fa387e2) (Urgent). This is the piece that persists entity-level meaning onto checkpoints and a git-native index, so meaning is stored with git history and not re-parsed on demand. Done when entire and MCP can answer \\\"which checkpoints changed entity X\\\" from the index.\\n\\nEntity deltas in checkpoint metadata (target 2026-08-15): [COR-781](https://linear.app/entirehq/issue/COR-781/spike-map-semantic-extractor-snapshot-checkpoint-metadata-schema) spike the schema, then [COR-782](https://linear.app/entirehq/issue/COR-782/embed-entity-deltas-into-the-checkpoint-metadata-write-path) embed the deltas into the write path.\\n\\nGit-native entity to checkpoint index (target 2026-09-15): [COR-783](https://linear.app/entirehq/issue/COR-783/build-the-git-native-entity-checkpoint-index) build the index, [COR-784]...\",\"createdTime\":\"2026-07-09T01:18:49Z\",\"fileExtension\":\"docx\",\"fileSize\":\"517504\",\"id\":\"REDACTED\",\"mimeType\":\"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document\",\"modifiedTime\":\"2026-07-10T02:00:40.110Z\",\"owner\":\"suhaan@entire.io\",\"sharedWithMeTime\":\"2026-07-09T19:56:46.035Z\",\"title\":\"Semantic Reasoning Layer - Roadmap.docx\",\"viewUrl\":\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view?usp=drivesdk\",\"viewedByMeTime\":\"2026-07-09T20:01:19.867Z\"},{\"canAddChildren\":false,\"contentSnippet\":\"Harvard Undergraduate\\n\\nOpen Bio\\n\\nLaboratory\\n\\nPRESENTS\\n\\nSRI\\n\\nSTUDENT RESEARCH INSTITUTE\\n\\nISSUE 2 SUMMER '25\\n\\n2\\n\\nTable of Contents\\n\\nORIGINAL RESEARCH\\n\\nCUPNavigator: A multi-model CDSS for tumor origin prediction, diagnostic planning, and therapy recommendations in cancer of unknown primary Saicharan Vellanki and Salomon Kobongo\\n\\nDROID: Dual-headed Representation for Omics-based Identification of SARS-CoV-2 Infection and Cell type via Deep Learning Enan Khan and Tuan Pham\\n\\nIntegrative lipidomics and network pharmacology reveal the mechanisms of cannabidiol in the treatment of acute myeloid leukemia Aiden Yoon and Tomiwa Oyedokun\\n\\nBi-stable Auxetic Stent Design for Customized Vascular Conformability Tarun Malarvasan and Hrishikesh Kanade\\n\\nDecoding the dark proteome: Deep learning-enabled discovery of druggable enzymes in Wuchereria bancrofti Shawnak Shivakumar and Jefferson Hernandez\\n\\nLITERATURE REVIEW\\n\\nThe potential of CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing technology to enhance CAR T-cell therapy as a targeted treatment for leukemia Adwita Jagannathan and Daniella Lu\\n\\nReassessing the Undruggable: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of KRAS G12C Inhibitor Efficacy in 2,031 Cancer Patients Suchir Ryali and Andrew Evans\\n\\nInterplay of Microglia and the Glymphatic system in Alzheimer’s Disease A Systematic Review on the Crosstalk of Microglia and the Glymphatic System in Alzheimer’s Disease Dhriti Kantipudi and Hannah Yoder\\n\\nThe dialect of chromatin in chronic pain and its distortion under opioid exposure Sahana Sivam and Carlos Rivera\\n\\nThe role of stress-related biomolecules in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma: A tidal wave of consequences\\n\\nVivian Zhao and Brielle Patlin\\n\\nCONFERENCE PROCEEDINGS\\n\\nSTEM Fellowship Journal vol.0 ● issue 0\\n\\n3\\n\\nLetter Letter from from the the Harvard Harvard Undergraduate Undergraduate OpenBio OpenBio Laboratory Laboratory Student Student Research Research Institute Institute Team Team\\n\\nDear Readers,\\n\\nIt is a joy and an honor to share this issue of STEM Fellowship Journal, encapsulating the inquisitiveness, dedication, and commitment to science of the Student Research Institute (SRI) Summer 2025 cohort. Within these pages, you will find the work of students who remind us why science matters and why access to it must be protected and expanded. The student researchers of this second cohort of SRI have shown us that curiosity knows no boundaries of geography, background, or circumstance.\\n\\nSRI was created with the simple belief that every young person with a question burning inside them deserves the chance to explore it. Every student who dreams of research deserves a seat at the table, regardless of whether their school has a lab, their family can pay for a program, or they even know what “research” looks like. This summer, more than 2,000 students from 38 countries reached out with that dream. One hundred and fifteen were selected to embark on an eight-week journey guided by mentors from 32 universities. Together, they explored fields from CRISPR-CAR T-cell therapy to tumor origin prediction. They read, they asked, they struggled, they discovered. And in the end, they produced papers and posters that reflect not only what they learned but who they are becoming as scientists.\\n\\nNone of this would have been possible without the extraordinary mentors who gave their time and care. Balancing their own demanding research, they chose to teach, to guide, and to believe in the potential of students they had never met before. Their generosity created spaces where curiosity could take root and where confidence could begin to grow. We are deeply grateful for their example.\\n\\nThe pages that follow are filled with the beginnings of scientific journeys. Yet they are more than first steps. They are reminders of what science can be when access is widened and voices once overlooked are invited in. They are proof that talent is everywhere, waiting for an open door.\\n\\nAs you read, we invite you not only to celebrate the accomplishments of these young researchers but also to imagine what it means for the future of science when they are given the tools to thrive. The discoveries they make in the years ahead may change fields we cannot yet name. What matters now is that they have begun, and that they know they belong here.\\n\\nOn behalf of the Harvard Undergraduate OpenBio Laboratory, we extend our heartfelt congratulations to this year’s students, our gratitude to their mentors, and our hope for all the journeys that lie ahead. The future of science is undoubtedly brighter because of them.\\n\\nSahil Sood Co-Director of Engagement, SRI Co-Lead , Harvard Undergraduate OpenBio Laboratory\\n\\nAlex Merheb Co-Director of Engagement, SRI Co-Lead, Harvard Undergraduate OpenBio Laboratory\\n\\nSean Meng Co-President, Harvard Undergraduate OpenBio Laboratory\\n\\nHugh Hankenson Co-President, Harvard Undergraduate OpenBio Laboratory\\n\\n2026 2026 journal.stemfellowship.org journal.stemfellowship.org\\n\\n4 ORIGINAL INVESTIGATIONS\\n\\n1ST PLACE, ORIGINAL RESEARC...\",\"createdTime\":\"2026-03-26T20:17:33.898Z\",\"fileExtension\":\"pdf\",\"fileSize\":\"9015122\",\"id\":\"REDACTED\",\"mimeType\":\"application/pdf\",\"modifiedTime\":\"2026-03-26T20:19:07.509Z\",\"owner\":\"ashao@college.harvard.edu\",\"sharedWithMeTime\":\"2026-04-22T21:44:48.287Z\",\"title\":\"SRI_Issue_V3.pdf\",\"viewUrl\":\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view?usp=drivesdk\",\"viewedByMeTime\":\"2026-05-27T01:57:04.734Z\"}]}","mcpMeta":{"structuredContent":{"files":[{"canAddChildren":false,"contentSnippet":"# ð§  The Semantic Reasoning Layer: Roadmap\n\nTurn the local brain into a shared reasoning substrate that a fleet of agents builds on. Git preserves change, but it does not preserve meaning. This initiative gives every agent persistent, shared, pluggable memory tied to git history, so humans and agents can reason and build in parallel without colliding.\n\nInitiative: [Semantic Reasoning Layer](https://linear.app/entirehq/initiative/semantic-reasoning-layer-e043385c2167) (Linear, Active) · Vision: [entire.io/vision](https://entire.io/vision) · As of: July 2026\n\nThe arc: ð¢ P0 finish the brain, then ðµ P1 host it, then ð£ P2 unify memory behind one surface, then ð  P3 coordinate fleets and ð´ P4 federate across repos in parallel, all feeding ⭐ P5, the north star: behavioral intelligence, where retained behavior becomes decisions (best-agent routing, prompt quality per person, a best-prompt library).\n\nGoverning constraint: no new infrastructure. The whole arc rides entire.io's existing rails (entiredb, checkpoint\\_remote, snapshotstore and snapshotcrypt, authz and SpiceDB, the jurisdiction forwarder). Each milestone ports proven parts onto the brain model rather than building storage or crypto from scratch.\n\n## ð Timeline at a glance\n\nTarget: the whole roadmap complete by end of March 2027. The schedule below is the compressed plan to hit that date. The dates run tight and in parallel from P2 onward.\n\n| Phase | Focus | Window | Status |\n| :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| ð¢ P0 GA the local brain | Harden entire-sem, brain, MCP surface, bench | Jul to Aug 2026 | Ships today, GA wrapper in progress |\n| ðµ P1 Shared / hosted brain | Server store, sync, remote query, auth | Now, ship Aug 2026 | Built and tested on clones, one step from prod |\n| ð§© Keystone: semantic index tied to VCS | Entity deltas, git-native index, causality | Aug to Sep 2026 | Planned |\n| ð£ P2 Unified memory, one MCP surface | Converge search, one backbone, one surface | Sep to Nov 2026 | Planned |\n| ð¡ Reasoning capture, compounding memory | Record schema, write-back, provenance graph | Oct to Dec 2026 | Planned |\n| ð  P3 Coordinated agents | Claims, conflict detection, handovers, scheduler | Nov 2026 to Feb 2027 | Planned |\n| ð´ P4 Cross-repo federation | Federated discovery, capability registry | Jan to Mar 2027 | Planned |\n| ⭐ P5 Behavioral intelligence (north star) | Prompt quality, best-prompt library, fit, auto-routing | Jan to Jun 2027 | Planned |\n\nThe March 2027 target covers the enabling phases (P0 through P4). The P5 north star starts as soon as P2's surface and P3's coordination land, and runs to June 2027. Linear milestone dates now match the compressed schedule.\n\n## ð¢ P0, GA the local brain\n\nWhat it is. The foundation the whole initiative stands on. The local brain already runs, so P0 is finish work and release hygiene, not new build. There is no gate here. Everything ships today, but nothing is formally tagged, so P0 is the wrapper that makes it something the rest of the arc can depend on.\n\nThe pieces, all live today.\n\nentire-sem is the semantic provider. It parses code into symbols and relations with tree-sitter, and it uses no vectors anywhere in the stack.\n\nentire-brain is the local brain. It holds durable, branch-scoped, provenance-anchored facts, plus a local MCP surface of about 33 tools that agents query.\n\nbrain-bench is the CI gate. It benchmarks the extractor against a reference tool across roughly 30 languages and fails the build on regression or drift.\n\nThe milestones.\n\nM1, harden and GA entire-sem. Version-stamp it and freeze the schema so everything downstream can trust the contract.\n\nM2, close the brain blockers. Ship the local brain with honest claims, no overreach.\n\nM3, GA the MCP surface for both plugins. Harden the local tool surface for the Claude Code and Codex plugins.\n\nM4, brain-bench as the automated eval gate. Make the benchmark a required regression floor.\n\nM5, release logistics. The release checklist, the compatibility matrix, and distribution.\n\nFoundation build tracked in Linear: [Semantic Index Tied to VCS History](https://linear.app/entirehq/project/semantic-index-tied-to-vcs-history-ea453fa387e2) (Urgent). This is the piece that persists entity-level meaning onto checkpoints and a git-native index, so meaning is stored with git history and not re-parsed on demand. Done when entire and MCP can answer \"which checkpoints changed entity X\" from the index.\n\nEntity deltas in checkpoint metadata (target 2026-08-15): [COR-781](https://linear.app/entirehq/issue/COR-781/spike-map-semantic-extractor-snapshot-checkpoint-metadata-schema) spike the schema, then [COR-782](https://linear.app/entirehq/issue/COR-782/embed-entity-deltas-into-the-checkpoint-metadata-write-path) embed the deltas into the write path.\n\nGit-native entity to checkpoint index (target 2026-09-15): [COR-783](https://linear.app/entirehq/issue/COR-783/build-the-git-native-entity-checkpoint-index) build the index, [COR-784]...","createdTime":"2026-07-09T01:18:49Z","fileExtension":"docx","fileSize":"517504","id":"1sVWKzuP_sQGpf7l8M2Yf_hgJE2kOgHvW","mimeType":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","modifiedTime":"2026-07-10T02:00:40.110Z","owner":"suhaan@entire.io","sharedWithMeTime":"2026-07-09T19:56:46.035Z","title":"Semantic Reasoning Layer - Roadmap.docx","viewUrl":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view?usp=drivesdk","viewedByMeTime":"2026-07-09T20:01:19.867Z"},{"canAddChildren":false,"contentSnippet":"Harvard Undergraduate\n\nOpen Bio\n\nLaboratory\n\nPRESENTS\n\nSRI\n\nSTUDENT RESEARCH INSTITUTE\n\nISSUE 2 SUMMER '25\n\n2\n\nTable of Contents\n\nORIGINAL RESEARCH\n\nCUPNavigator: A multi-model CDSS for tumor origin prediction, diagnostic planning, and therapy recommendations in cancer of unknown primary Saicharan Vellanki and Salomon Kobongo\n\nDROID: Dual-headed Representation for Omics-based Identification of SARS-CoV-2 Infection and Cell type via Deep Learning Enan Khan and Tuan Pham\n\nIntegrative lipidomics and network pharmacology reveal the mechanisms of cannabidiol in the treatment of acute myeloid leukemia Aiden Yoon and Tomiwa Oyedokun\n\nBi-stable Auxetic Stent Design for Customized Vascular Conformability Tarun Malarvasan and Hrishikesh Kanade\n\nDecoding the dark proteome: Deep learning-enabled discovery of druggable enzymes in Wuchereria bancrofti Shawnak Shivakumar and Jefferson Hernandez\n\nLITERATURE REVIEW\n\nThe potential of CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing technology to enhance CAR T-cell therapy as a targeted treatment for leukemia Adwita Jagannathan and Daniella Lu\n\nReassessing the Undruggable: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of KRAS G12C Inhibitor Efficacy in 2,031 Cancer Patients Suchir Ryali and Andrew Evans\n\nInterplay of Microglia and the Glymphatic system in Alzheimer’s Disease A Systematic Review on the Crosstalk of Microglia and the Glymphatic System in Alzheimer’s Disease Dhriti Kantipudi and Hannah Yoder\n\nThe dialect of chromatin in chronic pain and its distortion under opioid exposure Sahana Sivam and Carlos Rivera\n\nThe role of stress-related biomolecules in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma: A tidal wave of consequences\n\nVivian Zhao and Brielle Patlin\n\nCONFERENCE PROCEEDINGS\n\nSTEM Fellowship Journal vol.0 ● issue 0\n\n3\n\nLetter Letter from from the the Harvard Harvard Undergraduate Undergraduate OpenBio OpenBio Laboratory Laboratory Student Student Research Research Institute Institute Team Team\n\nDear Readers,\n\nIt is a joy and an honor to share this issue of STEM Fellowship Journal, encapsulating the inquisitiveness, dedication, and commitment to science of the Student Research Institute (SRI) Summer 2025 cohort. Within these pages, you will find the work of students who remind us why science matters and why access to it must be protected and expanded. The student researchers of this second cohort of SRI have shown us that curiosity knows no boundaries of geography, background, or circumstance.\n\nSRI was created with the simple belief that every young person with a question burning inside them deserves the chance to explore it. Every student who dreams of research deserves a seat at the table, regardless of whether their school has a lab, their family can pay for a program, or they even know what “research” looks like. This summer, more than 2,000 students from 38 countries reached out with that dream. One hundred and fifteen were selected to embark on an eight-week journey guided by mentors from 32 universities. Together, they explored fields from CRISPR-CAR T-cell therapy to tumor origin prediction. They read, they asked, they struggled, they discovered. And in the end, they produced papers and posters that reflect not only what they learned but who they are becoming as scientists.\n\nNone of this would have been possible without the extraordinary mentors who gave their time and care. Balancing their own demanding research, they chose to teach, to guide, and to believe in the potential of students they had never met before. Their generosity created spaces where curiosity could take root and where confidence could begin to grow. We are deeply grateful for their example.\n\nThe pages that follow are filled with the beginnings of scientific journeys. Yet they are more than first steps. They are reminders of what science can be when access is widened and voices once overlooked are invited in. They are proof that talent is everywhere, waiting for an open door.\n\nAs you read, we invite you not only to celebrate the accomplishments of these young researchers but also to imagine what it means for the future of science when they are given the tools to thrive. The discoveries they make in the years ahead may change fields we cannot yet name. What matters now is that they have begun, and that they know they belong here.\n\nOn behalf of the Harvard Undergraduate OpenBio Laboratory, we extend our heartfelt congratulations to this year’s students, our gratitude to their mentors, and our hope for all the journeys that lie ahead. The future of science is undoubtedly brighter because of them.\n\nSahil Sood Co-Director of Engagement, SRI Co-Lead , Harvard Undergraduate OpenBio Laboratory\n\nAlex Merheb Co-Director of Engagement, SRI Co-Lead, Harvard Undergraduate OpenBio Laboratory\n\nSean Meng Co-President, Harvard Undergraduate OpenBio Laboratory\n\nHugh Hankenson Co-President, Harvard Undergraduate OpenBio Laboratory\n\n2026 2026 journal.stemfellowship.org journal.stemfellowship.org\n\n4 ORIGINAL INVESTIGATIONS\n\n1ST PLACE, ORIGINAL RESEARC...","createdTime":"2026-03-26T20:17:33.898Z","fileExtension":"pdf","fileSize":"9015122","id":"1-4Aa8T0U4b2CjhZYtObx0Xe5jThEMFYZ","mimeType":"application/pdf","modifiedTime":"2026-03-26T20:19:07.509Z","owner":"ashao@college.harvard.edu","sharedWithMeTime":"2026-04-22T21:44:48.287Z","title":"SRI_Issue_V3.pdf","viewUrl":"https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view?usp=drivesdk","viewedByMeTime":"2026-05-27T01:57:04.734Z"}]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"36c85f1f-c59c-4f7a-8d42-aa60376bc9c2","session_id":"f507d58c-285f-4d11-b1d0-201f2a5e172d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/p0p1/api-242","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.233","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
