 150-300 sealed pairs; a power analysis from pilot variance choosing final n; justify n statistically in the paper.\n2. CONTAMINATION: (a) discussion + per-repo breakdowns; (b) a post-training-cutoff FRESH pair subset (SWE-rebench-style mining of recent PRs, or SWE-bench-verified date filtering) as a robustness split; (c) argue why the PAIRED design partially controls memorization (both arms see the same B task; memory delta is the measurand) — but verify this argument holds.\n3. GENERALITY: ≥2 agent backends (claude + codex/gpt-5.6-terra via Azure; the run.py codex wiring exists) and ≥2 reps per cell or a variance-decomposition justification (we have prior evidence run-level noise dominates instance identity).\n4. CONSTRUCT VALIDITY of locate_calls_pre_edit: human-label a sample (~30 B sessions) for \"re-derivation of session-A knowledge\", report correlation; pre-register the validation protocol.\n5. SEAL PROTOCOL: 2 independent raters + Cohen's kappa + documented criteria + adjudication; raters cannot be the harness authors ideally (or disclose).\n6. CONTROLS: add an exploratory \"irrelevant-memory\" control arm (packet from an UNRELATED session A, same size) — distinguishes relevant-memory value from placebo-context effects; null test already planned.\n7. BASELINE DIGNITY: competitor best-config protocol — document each competitor's config selection procedure (their published defaults + one tuning pass on dev split only), pin versions.\n8. ARTIFACTS: croissant + RAI metadata generation script; anonymized release repo (\"BrainMark\" neutral naming, memory systems as pluggable adapters); HF dataset hosting; Zenodo DOI; maintenance/versioning policy; leaderboard plan; MIT licensing chain (SWE-bench is MIT — verify each pool's license).\n9. PAPER SKELETON: contributions framing (first sealed cross-session memory-utility benchmark for coding agents + mechanism metric + N-system comparison), related-work map (LoCoMo, LongMemEval, mem0/Letta evals, SWE-bench family, agent-memory surveys), limitations, ethics, compute disclosure, NeurIPS checklist pass.\n10. TIMELINE/BUDGET: NeurIPS 2027 submission (abstract ~May 2027). Phase the spend: current pilot ladder unchanged; full-scale runs sized for the bigger n and 2 backends — estimate honestly (cheap-model tier vs Opus tier).\n\nOUTPUT: a concrete phased implementation plan (what to build/change in the brainmark/ tree, what new scripts/docs, what runs, what human protocols), each phase with cost + wall-clock + owner (agent-automatable vs needs-human), ordered so $0 items front-load. Include a Verification section (how we know each gap is closed) and an honest \"residual acceptance risks we cannot engineer away\" list. Max ~110 dense lines.","outputFile":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-devenv/f507d58c-285f-4d11-b1d0-201f2a5e172d/tasks/a102d538487b6c8e3.output","canReadOutputFile":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"645c25ee-dbf0-44a8-9af5-874c975eb05f","session_id":"f507d58c-285f-4d11-b1d0-201f2a5e172d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.233","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
