{"parentUuid":"58001ac7-02c8-4553-aa29-5961c3ad3931","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4dbe3451-088a-4a0d-a415-ecd385481d0d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"k go ahead on that and udap telinear accordingly here si the plan btw if u forgot; 🧠 The Semantic Reasoning Layer: Roadmap\nWhile version control preserves change, it does not preserve meaning. And without shared context, agents cannot collaborate and build together. We will unlock agent-to-agent collaboration with a semantic reasoning layer directly tied to version control history, giving every agent persistent, shared, and pluggable memory. This preserves context so groups of humans and fleets of agents can reason, coordinate, and build together in parallel, without overwriting, colliding, or losing understanding. And because every prompt, session, and outcome is retained, the layer learns the organization itself: which prompts succeed, which people and agents are best at which work, and it routes each task to whoever, human or agent, will do it best.\nInitiative: Semantic Reasoning Layer (Linear, Active) · Vision: entire.io/vision · As of: July 2026\n\nHow this plan flows\nOne sentence per phase, in the order they unlock each other:\n🟢 P0 releases the brain people can install (it works today, but it is still private).\n🔵 P1 hosts it so a team shares one brain instead of one per laptop.\n🧩 The keystone ties meaning to git history so every checkpoint knows which entities it changed.\n🟣 P2 unifies memory: today four search systems, three embedding backbones, and two extractors exist; P2 collapses them onto one surface.\n🟡 Reasoning capture makes understanding compound: reviews and syntheses write facts back instead of emitting prose that scrolls away.\n🟠 P3 coordinates fleets (entity claims, pre-merge conflict detection) and 🔴 P4 federates across repos, in parallel.\n⭐ P5 is the payoff: prompt quality per person, a best-prompt library, agent and person to task fit, auto-routing.\nEvery overlap with existing systems is resolved as a named milestone inside a phase, not as a side conversation. The rule throughout: no new infrastructure; ride entiredb, checkpoints, snapshotcrypt, and SpiceDB.\nMilestone status legend: ✅ done · 🔨 built, not shipped · 🔄 pending.\n\nWhere we stand, honestly\n🟢 P0: built and gated, NOT finished. Both repos are tagged v0.1.0, the schema is frozen, brain-bench gates CI (better on 28 of 30 languages; beats codebase-memory-mcp 1,215 to 822 across 73 repos). But the repos are still private. A brain nobody outside the team can install is not released. P0 closes on release.\n🔵 P1: built and reviewed, NOT shipped. All four core milestones are code-complete on clone branches, race-clean, hardened by a four-angle cleanup pass, and the AI review shows 0 open findings. Not merged; prod is one env var away after merge. Not yet integrated with James's connector; that is a named milestone below.\nEverything downstream is scoped in Linear with owners and dates.\n\n🗺️ The landscape: five systems, five decisions, five milestones\nGrounded in full reads of each codebase. Each decision is a work item in this plan, not an intention.\nSystem (owner)\nThe overlap\nThe decision\nIn the plan as\nentirehq/mcp (James)\nP1's remote transport duplicates his solved OAuth/tenancy connector\nThe brain ships no second connector; it registers a tool surface on his server, our endpoint is the backend\n🔵 P1.M6\nperegrine (Evis)\nBoth peregrine and entire-sem maintain per-language tree-sitter extractors\nentire-sem becomes peregrine's precision symbol producer via its reserved SymbolSource::Scip slots; one extractor, two engines\n🟣 P2.M1b\nentire-search (Alisha)\nBoth consume checkpoints; otherwise disjoint (her vectors over prose, our structure and facts)\nDistilled brain facts become search documents in her index, a feed, not a fight\n🟣 P2.M5\nlibrarian (Alex)\nIts synthesis roadmap heads toward the same durable-facts territory over the same stream\nAgree the facts-layer boundary before either side builds further\n🟡 RC.M0\nMemoryRecords (Alisha, cli#765 proto)\nSame checkpoint stream, same outcome-mining territory as the north star\nP5 proceeds as a merged effort, not a third parallel one\n⭐ P5.M0\n\nPlus the storage decision that spans them: push the brain's storage down into EntireDB (blobs to objstore, fact CAS to refstore, audit to rsl), owned in decentralized-architecture with the brain as first customer (🔵 P1.M7). Per-object encryption and crypto-shred exist because the current store is one bucket shared across tenants; under the repo's own trust boundary that need mostly falls away.\n\n📅 Timeline at a glance\nEnablers (P0 through P4) complete by end of March 2027; the north star lands by June 2027.\nPhase\nFocus\nWindow\nStatus\n🟢 P0 Release the local brain\nRepos are private; release + distribution\nJul 2026\nBuilt + gated; release pending\n🔵 P1 Shared / hosted brain\nShip, surface on mcp, storage RFD\nAug to Oct 2026\nBuilt on clones; ship pending\n🧩 Keystone: semantic index tied to git\nEntity deltas, git-native index, causality\nAug to Sep 2026\nPlanned\n🟣 P2 Unified memory, one MCP surface\nOne engine, one backbone, one surface\nSep to Dec 2026\nPlanned\n🟡 Reasoning capture\nAlignment, record schema, write-back, provenance\nOct to Dec 2026\nPlanned\n🟠 P3 Coordinated agents\nClaims, conflict detection, handovers, scheduler\nNov 2026 to Feb 2027\nPlanned\n🔴 P4 Cross-repo federation\nFederated discovery, capability registry\nJan to Mar 2027\nPlanned\n⭐ P5 Behavioral intelligence\nPrompt quality, fit, auto-routing\nJan to Jun 2027\nPlanned\n\n\n🟢 P0, release the local brain\nWhat it is. The foundation. The code is done and proven: entire-sem (tree-sitter structural extraction, symbols plus a 30-type relation graph, no vectors anywhere), entire-brain (durable facts distilled from sessions, anchored to commit and transcript line, served over about 33 MCP tools), and brain-bench (the CI gate). What is NOT done is the release: both repos are still private. A memory layer for agents that no one can install is a prototype with good hygiene, not a shipped foundation.\n✅ M1, harden entire-sem. Tagged v0.1.0, version stamped from the tag, schema 1.x frozen (ADR 0001, additive minors, tolerant readers).\n✅ M2, close the brain blockers. Shipped internally with honest claims.\n✅ M3, the local MCP surface. The ~33 tools serve, verified independent.\n✅ M4, brain-bench as the CI gate. 28 of 30 languages, byte-identical no-drift rule; the competitive eval beats codebase-memory-mcp 1,215 to 822 (91% vs 62%) across 73 real repositories.\n🔄 M5, release + distribution (target 2026-07-31). Make the repos available beyond the team, an install path, docs, the announcement. P0 is done when someone outside the team can install and run it.\nTracked: P0 · GA the Local Brain (In Progress).\n\n🔵 P1, the shared / hosted brain ⭐\nWhat it is. The differentiator: lift the brain off the laptop so one member's agent learns something and every member's agent can read it. The core is code-complete and reviewed; what remains is shipping it and wiring it into the platform instead of beside it.\nBuilt (on clone branches, race-clean, 0 open review findings):\n🔨 M1, server-side brain store. Content-addressed, authz-gated, encrypted at rest: fresh per-object DEK, AES-256-GCM, KMS-wrapped, AAD-bound, crypto-shred on retract so a removed fact is provably unreadable.\n🔨 M2, durable-fact sync. Compare-and-swap on the branch head, injective slug@ULID tenancy key, 412 on a lost race, tamper-evident hash-chained audit log.\n🔨 M3, multi-machine shared query. HTTP JSON-RPC endpoint serving the curated facts tools remotely. This endpoint is the backend of M6; it is not its own connector.\n🔨 M4, auth and tenancy. Two independent fail-closed isolation layers on the org, project, repo SpiceDB cascade.\nRemaining (the integration milestones):\n🔄 M5, ship to production (target 2026-08-31). Merge PR #242 and PR #65; staging deploys automatically (additive migrations); set ENTIRE_BRAIN_HOSTED_ENABLED=true on US and EU staging; smoke the full round trip on partial.to (publish, two-member CAS advance, remote read, audit chain, crypto-shred); then the same flag on prod. Rollback is unsetting the flag: endpoints 503, data stays sealed. No new bucket, key, IAM, or credentials; it reuses session-share's live cipher, bucket, and KMS.\n🔄 M6, brain tool surface on entirehq/mcp (target 2026-09-15). Register the brain tools on James's hosted connector. One connector for clients, zero duplicate auth plumbing. Not integrated yet; this is the work item.\n🔄 M7, EntireDB storage push-down RFD (target 2026-10-15). With decentralized-architecture: blobs to objstore, fact CAS to refstore, audit to rsl, aligned with the git-meta direction. The organizing rule: the brain owns the what and the when; decentralized-architecture owns the shared rails, because a capability with many consumers lives with the platform, not inside one product.\nLinks: P1 project · Trail #243 (0 open findings).\n\n🧩 The keystone: semantic index tied to git history\nWhat it is. The piece the vision names directly: persist entity-level meaning onto checkpoints and a git-native index, so meaning is stored with version-control history and not re-parsed on demand. Done when entire and MCP can answer \"which checkpoints changed entity X.\"\n🔄 Entity deltas in checkpoint metadata (2026-08-15): COR-781 spike the schema, COR-782 embed the deltas in the write path.\n🔄 Git-native entity to checkpoint index (2026-09-15): COR-783 build it, COR-784 expose it over MCP.\n🔄 Transparency-log semantic causality (2026-09-30): COR-785 ref-change events carry why, not just that.\nTracked: Semantic Index Tied to VCS History (Urgent).\n\n🟣 P2, unified memory: one MCP surface\nWhat it is. The cleanup and the chokepoint. Today: four search systems live or half-live (Alisha's entire-search, Evis's peregrine, Alex's librarian, plus an archived scaffold), three embedding backbones (Cohere 1536, nomic 768, none), two tree-sitter extractors. P2 collapses them into one shared memory on one backbone behind one MCP surface that every agent queries. Done when one MCP tool returns unified results and only one engine and one backbone remain in production.\n🔄 M1, converge code search on one engine (2026-09-30): COR-786 the RFD, COR-787 ship it.\n🔄 M1b, entire-sem feeds peregrine's symbol overlay (2026-10-15). Kill the duplicate extractors: entire-sem becomes the precision producer for peregrine's reserved SymbolSource::Scip and SymbolPrecision::Semantic slots. Peregrine stays find-text-fast; the brain stays understand-structure.\n🔄 M2, one embedding backbone (2026-10-31): Cohere 1536 (entire-search) vs nomic 768 (librarian): pick one. COR-788\n🔄 M3, the single MCP shared-memory surface (2026-11-30), living on entirehq/mcp: COR-789\n🔄 M4, retire duplicates, prove no regression: COR-790\n🔄 M5, brain facts feed entire-search (2026-12-15). The checkpoint seam becomes a feed: distilled facts are better search documents than raw transcript chunks. Gated on P1 shipping and the facts-layer alignment.\nTracked: Unified, Pluggable Agent Memory (High).\n\n🟡 Reasoning capture and compounding memory\nWhat it is. Make understanding compound. Today reviews, syntheses, and assistants emit prose that gets overwritten, so agents restart every run. This captures reasoning as structured records that write back into shared memory. Done when a second run demonstrably reuses the first run's reasoning.\n🔄 M0, facts-layer alignment (2026-10-15). Brain durable facts × librarian's synthesis roadmap × Alisha's MemoryRecords: three derivers over one checkpoint stream agree an owned boundary each and one shared record contract, before schema work.\n🔄 M1, structured reasoning-record schema (2026-10-31): COR-791, COR-792\n🔄 M2, write-back from reasoning surfaces (2026-11-30): review findings become durable facts. COR-793\n🔄 M3, queryable provenance graph (2026-12-31): COR-794\nTracked: Reasoning Capture and Compounding Memory (High).\n\n🟠 P3, coordinated agents\nWhat it is. Fleets that build in parallel without colliding: today two agents editing the same function silently collide. Done when concurrent edits to one entity warn before merge, ready tasks auto-dispatch, and handovers auto-hydrate.\n🔄 M1, entity-level claims and leases (2026-11-30): COR-795, COR-796\n🔄 M2, pre-merge semantic conflict detection (2026-12-31): COR-797\n🔄 M3, structured handovers and auto-hydration (2027-01-31): COR-798, COR-799\n🔄 M4, scheduler to sandboxes (2027-02-28): COR-800\nTracked: Semantic Collision-Free Parallel Agents (High).\n\n🔴 P4, cross-repo federation\nWhat it is. Break the repo boundary: an agent in repo A surfaces relevant reasoning from repo B, and tasks route by declared capability.\n🔄 M1, cross-repo semantic discovery (2027-02-28): COR-801\n🔄 M2, cross-org authz: COR-802\n🔄 M3, agent capability registry (2027-03-31): COR-803\nTracked: Cross-Repo Shared Reasoning (Medium).\n\n⭐ P5, behavioral intelligence: the north star\nWhat it is. The payoff the arc builds toward: turn the retained record into decisions. Which prompts succeed. Which employees write prompts that work, and the best of their prompts as a shared library. Who, or which agent, is best at what. Route work accordingly, and let the org watch itself learn. Origin: the Salesforce ask. Gated on P2's surface (to read the record) and P3's coordination (to route the work).\n🔄 M0, merge-of-efforts alignment with MemoryRecords (2027-01-15). Alisha's prototype already mines this territory; P5 merges with it rather than paralleling it. The brain brings provenance-anchored facts and brain-bench's eval discipline as the scoring pattern.\n🔄 M1, prompt-quality classifier (2027-02-28): score prompts from retained outcome signal.\n🔄 M2, per-person profiles and the best-prompt library (2027-03-31).\n🔄 M3, agent and person to task fit (2027-04-30).\n🔄 M4, auto-routing (2027-05-31): P4's registry supplies the candidates.\n🔄 M5, feedback loop and org dashboards (2027-06-30).\nTracked: P5 · Behavioral Intelligence (North Star) (High).\n\nPrinciples\nOne connector. entirehq/mcp is the hosted MCP transport; everything registers surfaces there.\nOne extractor. entire-sem is the tree-sitter producer; peregrine consumes its precision overlay.\nOne backbone. P2.M2 picks the single embedding stack for prose; the brain itself stays vector-free.\nConsumers rule. A capability with many consumers (storage, crypto, git-meta) lives with the platform (decentralized-architecture); the brain drives the what and the when.\nThe index rebuilds; the facts endure. SQLite and embeddings are disposable derivations; durable facts and checkpoints are the record.\nEncryption is a property of where data sits. Per-object DEKs and crypto-shred exist because the store is shared across tenants; in-repo under the repo's own trust boundary the need mostly falls away.\nSchema discipline. major.minor, additive minors, tolerant readers (ADR 0001); opt-in egress, never implicit (ADR-P1-A); default push builds baseline, feature builds overlay, merge promotes, delete garbage-collects (ADR-P1-C).\n\nCritical path\nP0 releases the foundation. P1 is the linchpin: store, sync, query, auth, then the ship to prod and the surface on the one connector. The keystone and P2 land next, and P2.M3, the single surface, is the chokepoint every downstream tool registers on. Reasoning capture starts with the three-way facts alignment, then compounds. P3 and P4 run in parallel on P1 plus the surface. Everything converges on ⭐ P5, where the initiative pays for itself.\n\n🔗 Full tracking table\nPhase\nMilestone (target)\nWork\nStatus\n🟢 P0\nM1 to M4\ntags, schema freeze, MCP surface, bench gate\n✅\n🟢 P0\nM5 Release + distribution (2026-07-31)\nrepos public/org, install path, announce\n🔄\n🔵 P1\nM1 to M4\nstore, sync, query, tenancy (PR #242, PR #65)\n🔨\n🔵 P1\nM5 Ship to production (2026-08-31)\nmerge, staging, flag, smoke, prod\n🔄\n🔵 P1\nM6 Surface on entirehq/mcp (2026-09-15)\nregisterBrainTools on James's connector\n🔄\n🔵 P1\nM7 EntireDB push-down RFD (2026-10-15)\nobjstore/refstore/rsl with decentralized-architecture\n🔄\n🧩 Keystone\nEntity deltas (2026-08-15)\nCOR-781, COR-782\n🔄\n🧩 Keystone\nEntity index + MCP (2026-09-15)\nCOR-783, COR-784\n🔄\n🧩 Keystone\nLog causality (2026-09-30)\nCOR-785\n🔄\n🟣 P2\nOne engine (2026-09-30)\nCOR-786, COR-787\n🔄\n🟣 P2\nM1b sem feeds peregrine (2026-10-15)\nSymbolSource::Scip overlay\n🔄\n🟣 P2\nOne backbone (2026-10-31)\nCOR-788\n🔄\n🟣 P2\nOne surface (2026-11-30)\nCOR-789\n🔄\n🟣 P2\nRetire duplicates\nCOR-790\n🔄\n🟣 P2\nM5 Facts feed entire-search (2026-12-15)\ndistilled facts as search documents\n🔄\n🟡 RC\nM0 Facts-layer alignment (2026-10-15)\nbrain × librarian × MemoryRecords boundary\n🔄\n🟡 RC\nRecord schema (2026-10-31)\nCOR-791, COR-792\n🔄\n🟡 RC\nWrite-back (2026-11-30)\nCOR-793\n🔄\n🟡 RC\nProvenance graph (2026-12-31)\nCOR-794\n🔄\n🟠 P3\nClaims (2026-11-30)\nCOR-795, COR-796\n🔄\n🟠 P3\nConflict detection (2026-12-31)\nCOR-797\n🔄\n🟠 P3\nHandovers (2027-01-31)\nCOR-798, COR-799\n🔄\n🟠 P3\nScheduler (2027-02-28)\nCOR-800\n🔄\n🔴 P4\nDiscovery (2027-02-28)\nCOR-801, COR-802\n🔄\n🔴 P4\nRegistry (2027-03-31)\nCOR-803\n🔄\n⭐ P5\nM0 Merge with MemoryRecords (2027-01-15)\nalignment with Alisha\n🔄\n⭐ P5\nM1 to M5 (Feb to Jun 2027)\nclassifier, profiles + library, fit, routing, dashboards\n🔄\n\nTotals: 8 Linear projects, 37 milestones, 23 scoped issues (COR-781 through COR-803).\n\n🔗 Links\nInitiative: Semantic Reasoning Layer · Vision: entire.io/vision\nP1: entire-api PR #242 · entire-brain PR #65 · Trail #243\nBench: brain-bench · competitive scoreboard vs CBM\nProjects: P0 · P1 · Keystone · P2 · Reasoning Capture · P3 · P4 · P5\n\n\n"},"uuid":"38200d1c-a238-4ffa-8a67-40b43a75e47a","timestamp":"2026-08-19T17:40:30.999Z","permissionMode":"bypassPermissions","origin":{"kind":"human"},"promptSource":"typed","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/p0p1/brainmark","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.233","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
