ain__brain_get_code_snippet","mcp__entire-brain__brain_get_graph_schema","mcp__entire-brain__brain_impact","mcp__entire-brain__brain_index_repository","mcp__entire-brain__brain_index_status","mcp__entire-brain__brain_ingest_traces","mcp__entire-brain__brain_list_projects","mcp__entire-brain__brain_multi_get","mcp__entire-brain__brain_patterns","mcp__entire-brain__brain_patterns_status","mcp__entire-brain__brain_query","mcp__entire-brain__brain_query_graph","mcp__entire-brain__brain_refresh","mcp__entire-brain__brain_regressions","mcp__entire-brain__brain_review","mcp__entire-brain__brain_search","mcp__entire-brain__brain_search_code","mcp__entire-brain__brain_search_graph","mcp__entire-brain__brain_status","mcp__entire-brain__brain_tests","mcp__entire-brain__brain_trace_path","mcp__entire-brain__brain_vsearch","mcp__entire-brain__brain_workspace_graph","mcp__entire-brain__brain_workspace_regressions","mcp__entire-brain__brain_workspace_review"],"removedNames":[],"readdedNames":[],"pendingMcpServers":[]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"989c4853-97d4-425e-b43d-ab6db15ff6a4","timestamp":"2026-08-19T19:00:47.544Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.236","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
{"parentUuid":"989c4853-97d4-425e-b43d-ab6db15ff6a4","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"mcp_instructions_delta","addedNames":["context7"],"addedBlocks":["## context7\nUse this server to fetch current documentation whenever the user asks about a library, framework, SDK, API, CLI tool, or cloud service — even well-known ones like React, Next.js, Prisma, Express, Tailwind, Django, or Spring Boot. This includes API syntax, configuration, version migration, library-specific debugging, setup instructions, and CLI tool usage. Use even when you think you know the answer — your training data may not reflect recent changes. Prefer this over web search for library docs.\n\nDo not use for: refactoring, writing scripts from scratch, debugging business logic, code review, or general programming concepts."],"removedNames":[]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"7cce5947-46c1-4400-aefb-a48c43574836","timestamp":"2026-08-19T19:00:47.544Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.236","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
{"parentUuid":"7cce5947-46c1-4400-aefb-a48c43574836","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/suhaan/.claude/plans/quizzical-sniffing-quasar.md","snippet":"1\t# BrainMark — flagship benchmark for entire-brain (fair, all-competitor, publication-grade)\n2\t\n3\t## Context\n4\t\n5\tLoCoMo/LME only measure the brain's retrieval substrate over chat transcripts (tonight's runs:\n6\tbase terra 90.84, chrono sol 92.99, chrono terra 91.90 — engine credibility, not product proof).\n7\tThe product claim — **\"a second coding session reuses what the first learned\"** — has never been\n8\tmeasured. BrainMark is that measurement: agent does task A, memory is derived from A's session,\n9\tagent does related task B with memory vs without vs competitors. Built to survive publication\n10\treview (user is thinking NeurIPS-class venues): pre-registered primary metric, sealed tasks,\n11\texternal tasks + external grading, released harness.\n12\t\n13\tUser decisions: **all comp arms** (mem0 + Graphify + cmm; NO ablation arms — one true brain arm only, user 2026-08-19) ·\n14\t**SWE-bench mined pairs** as task source · **pilot → full** budget path.\n15\t\n16\t## Architecture\n17\t\n18\tNew thin orchestrator **`benchmarks/agent-brain/brainmark/`** (in the entire-brain repo,\n19\tbranch off main / on top of keystone), importing the existing `run.py` **as a library**\n20\t(it has a `__main__` guard at run.py:11130 — do not modify it) for its fairness primitives:\n21\tpacket bounding (:4731/:5014), delimiter guard (:4840), arm-neutral policy text (:6724),\n22\tsealed-bundle schema (:3923), env sanitization (:5108), sandbox-exec (:5124), pinned distill\n23\tdriver (:4578). GraphMark (`/Users/suhaan/devenv/graphmark/agentic-swebench`) supplies the\n24\tSWE-bench side as patterns/data, not a framework: task JSONs, repo-cache worktrees+locks\n25\t(run_3arm.sh:54-89), netjail, Docker grading (tools/grade_tag.sh), and vendored paired stats\n26\t(tools/metrics.py — log-ratios, geo-mean, median, 20k bootstrap CI, McNemar, session gates).\n27\t\n28\t## Components (new files under `benchmarks/agent-brain/brainmark/`)\n29\t\n30\t- `config.json` — graphmark root, repo-cache, model tiers, packet max_bytes, per-competitor\n31\t  pins (mem0 version/embedder; graphify + cmm pinned binaries), distill pin, concurrency=8, seeds.\n32\t- `mine_pairs.py` — offline $0 miner (below) → `candidates/*.json`.\n33\t- `seal.py` + `PREREGISTRATION.md` — human-review promotion to `tasks/sealed/` (n≥30) +\n34\t  disjoint 10-pair dev split; `SEAL-MANIFEST.json` = sha256 of every task + miner commit +\n35\t  prereg hash. Report refuses to aggregate on hash mismatch. Seal BEFORE first confirmatory run.\n36\t- `session_a.py` — session A runs ONCE per pair per tier (`claude -p` stream-JSON, isolated\n37\t  CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR); native claude-code JSONL harvested and sha256-pinned. Shared by ALL arms.\n38\t- `memsources/{empty,full_brain,mem0_source,graphify_source,cmm_source}.py` — each:\n39\t  pinned-A-bytes in → memory packet out, bounded/guarded by run.py primitives; the PACKET is\n40\t  sha256-pinned (not the store), so ingest nondeterminism can't create arm asymmetry.\n41\t  - `full_brain`: write A transcript to `<brainDir>/sessions/main/<id>.jsonl` + synthesized\n42\t    manifest (contract proven in internal/cli/distill_cmd_test.go:25-75) → pinned\n43\t    `entire-brain distill` + `refresh history` → ONE frozen `brain search/brief --json`,\n44\t    query = B's problem_statement.\n45\t  - `mem0/graphify/cmm`: same A bytes into each competitor (duck-typed add/search clients —\n46\t    mirror /Users/suhaan/devenv/eg-memharness/bench/memory/benchmarks/common/entire_client.py;\n47\t    graphify+cmm clients exist there to adapt), retrieval with the SAME query string and byte\n48\t    budget, rendered into the IDENTICAL envelope. Prep cost recorded separately, reported.\n49\t- `prompts.py` — one scaffold for all 5 arms; packet block present in every arm (no_brain gets\n50\t  a fixed \"(no prior-session memory available)\" sentinel); symmetry gate = sha256 of prompt\n51\t  with packet region replaced by `<PACKET>` must be identical across a pair's arms; enforced\n52\t  at launch and re-checked at report time.\n53\t- `run_b.py` — B driver: worktree at base_commit_B (graphmark lock pattern), netjail, run.py\n54\t  env-sanitization + sandbox, `claude -p` stream-JSON safe mode; per-cell results dir with\n55\t  cc_out.json, stream.jsonl, patch.diff, packet.sha256, prompt_sym.sha256; idempotent resume\n56\t  (skip iff usd>0 ∧ patch nonempty).\n57\t- `mechmetrics.py` — PRIMARY pre-registered metric: `locate_calls_pre_edit` = tool_use in\n58\t  {Read,Grep,Glob} ∪ {Bash matching ^(rg|grep|egrep|git grep|find|ls|cat|head|tail|ag)\\b}\n59\t  strictly before first {Edit,Write,MultiEdit,NotebookEdit}; no-edit sessions counted whole +\n60\t  flagged; ratios on (count+1). Secondary: tokens/usd (modelUsage), duration, num_turns.\n61\t- `grade.py` — wraps graphmark grade_tag.sh (Docker swebench.harness.run_evaluation); B graded\n62\t  by its own FAIL_TO_PASS. `vendor/graphmark_metrics.py` — pinned metrics.py copy w/ provenance\n63\t  header. `report.py` — REPORT.md/json: full_brain vs no_brain (headline), vs mem0/graphify/cmm (competitor table).\n64\t\n65\t## Pair mining (deterministic, $0)\n66\t\n67\tGroup graphmark `tasks/*.json` instances by repo, sort by created_at; for ordered (A,B):\n68\t≥1 overlapping file between patch_A and patch_B (diff --git paths) · `git merge-base\n69\t--is-ancestor base_A base_B` · score = Jaccard(files) + hunk-header symbol overlap (optional\n70\t`entire graph diff` entity rerank when available) · leakage screens: patch-body token overlap\n71\t< 0.8 (A must not BE B's fix), borderline flagged for seal review · one best A per B ·\n72\temit ≥40 candidates with full provenance.\n73\t\n74\t## Arm matrix (session A once per pair, pinned, shared by all arms)\n75\t\n76\t| arm | memory source | proves |\n77\t|---|---|---|\n78\t| no_brain | sentinel-empty packet | baseline |\n79\t| full_brain | distill+history over pinned A JSONL | the product (the ONLY brain arm) |\n80\t| mem0 | mem0 OSS over same bytes | vs the named competitor |\n81\t| graphify | graphify over same bytes | vs YC code-memory competitor |\n82\t| cmm | cmm over same bytes | vs codebase-memory-mcp |\n83\t\n84\tAll packets harness-delivered through one envelope (phase0b doctrine — self-retrieval arms\n85\tdeferred to v2 exploratory).\n86\t\n87\t## Gates (all vendored/enforced)\n88\t\n89\tPer-session usd>0 ∧ patch nonempty · all-arms-clean pair gate, symmetric drops · prompt-symmetry\n90\tsha equal · packet sha matches pin · no Entire-family tools outside full_brain · no\n91\t.entire/.benchmark/checkpoint-ref inspection anywhere (run.py audit) · no A-artifact paths\n92\treadable from B worktree.\n93\t\n94\t## Runbook + cost (5 arms)\n95\t\n96\t1. **$0**: mine → human seal (n≥30 + 10 dev) → prereg freeze → symmetry/pinning/miner-determinism\n97\t   unit tests.\n98\t2. **Null test (~$15, non-negotiable)**: 3 dev pairs × 2 reps, full_brain machinery with EMPTY\n99\t   packet vs no_brain — primary + token deltas must straddle 0. Proves the machinery is inert.\n100\t3. **Pilot (Sonnet, 10 dev pairs)**: 10 A + 50 B + prep ≈ **$60–120**. Fix harness only, never tasks.\n101\t4. **Full (n≥30 sealed, main model)**: 30 A + 150 B (5 arms) ≈ **$700–1,500 on Opus** (or ~$150–300 on\n102\t   gpt-5.6-class via Azure if the user prefers). Concurrency 8, resume-safe.\n103\t5. Docker grading (free) → `report.py`.\n104\t\n105\t## Verification\n106\t\n107\tMiner determinism (byte-identical double run) · seal-hash round-trip · symmetry sha across\n108\tstub-packet arms · delimiter-guard rejection test · packet re-derivation → identical sha ·\n109\tmechmetrics vs 3 hand-labeled stream fixtures · null test (above) · leakage audit (grep B\n110\ttranscripts for A instance_ids/paths; sandbox denials logged) · one-pair end-to-end smoke\n111\tincluding Docker grading — all BEFORE the full spend.\n112\t\n113\t## Critical reused files\n114\t\n115\t- brain-keystone `benchmarks/agent-brain/run.py` (library import; DO NOT modify)\n116\t- brain-keystone `benchmarks/agent-brain/CONDITIONS.md` (normative contract; extend, don't fork)\n117\t- graphmark `agentic-swebench/tools/metrics.py` (vendor), `run_3arm.sh` (patterns),\n118\t  `tools/grade_tag.sh` (grading), `tasks/*.json` (instance pool)\n119\t- brain-keystone `internal/cli/distill_cmd_test.go:25-75` (raw-JSONL ingest contract)\n\n... [89 lines truncated] ..."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"5063dd6c-a8dd-4d68-9102-19df88663281","timestamp":"2026-08-19T19:00:47.544Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.236","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
{"parentUuid":"5063dd6c-a8dd-4d68-9102-19df88663281","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/suhaan/.claude/projects/-Users-suhaan-devenv/memory/brainmark-state.md","snippet":"1\t---\n2\tname: brainmark-state\n3\tdescription: \"BrainMark (flagship A→B memory benchmark, NeurIPS 2027 target) — all $0 work done, PR #88 open; stopped at money line (null test $15 → pilot → confirmatory); seal session needs user + teammate\"\n4\tmetadata: \n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: project\n7\t  originSessionId: de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af\n8\t  modified: 2026-08-19T14:35:54.217Z\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\tBrainMark = sealed cross-session memory benchmark: task A → memory from A's pinned session bytes →\n12\trelated task B with memory vs without vs competitors. Plan: ~/.claude/plans/quizzical-sniffing-quasar.md.\n13\tWorktree p0p1/brainmark, branch feat/brainmark, **PR entireio/entire-brain#88** (10 commits, pushed).\n14\t\n15\tState 2026-08-19: ALL $0 work complete. 1,806 candidate pairs (SWE-bench+Verified+Multilingual,\n16\tpinned HF revisions; Multimodal excluded — unlicensed) + 79 fresh post-cutoff pairs (snapshot\n...\n16\tdual-rater seal w/ kappa, anonymizer 0-hit gate, croissant validates under real mlcroissant,\n17\tDocker grading smoke RESOLVED (arm64: manual `docker pull --platform linux/amd64` first —\n18\tdocumented in REPRODUCIBILITY.md). 331 tests green.\n19\t\n20\tNEXT (blocked on user): (1) seal session — user + one teammate, review/ packets ready,\n21\t~3.5-5.5h/rater per REVIEWING-QUICKSTART.md; (2) paid ladder, each rung needs GO: null test ~$15 →\n22\tpilot gpt-5.6-sol ~$60-120 → confirmatory ~$700-1,500 all-GPT. Never merge PR #88 without approval.\n23\tRelated: [[keystone-3-prs-open]], [[locomo-terra-result]].\n24\t\n25\t**PILOT DONE 2026-08-19 ($6.24 placeholder-priced, VM mem0harness-1):** null PASSED (after fixing a\n26\tpwd arm-name leak — null re-run); pilot n=20: full_brain locate −17.8% [CI incl 0], mem0 −1.4%,\n27\tresolved saturated 14-15/20 — direction favors brain, NOT significant by design. Power: 46 pairs\n28\t@2reps. Confirmatory projects ~$50-150 (burn ~$0.07/cell), not $700-1500. mem0 prep 20x slower\n29\t(58.8s vs 3.0s/pair). Six harness bugs found+fixed in pilot (dead primary metric via codex\n30\titem.type; silent-null memory arms via transcript format; distill billing public OpenAI; Azure\n31\tURL shape; mem0 undeployable defaults→forced deviation disclosed; worktree pwd leak). Fixes\n32\tbackported to both repos. BLOCKER: human seal session. Never quote pilot deltas as results."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"69ea1362-0602-4a37-a145-5a0b9288eb46","timestamp":"2026-08-19T19:00:47.544Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.236","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
{"parentUuid":"69ea1362-0602-4a37-a145-5a0b9288eb46","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- audit: Use when asked to \"/audit\", to audit code, or to make sure changes are bug-free. Runs a review-fix-reverify loop over the code from two lenses (normal code review AND adversarial bugbot-style hunting), fixing every issue, and repeating until two consecutive passes find nothing new with build/lint/tests green.\n- graph-bench-forensics: Measure whether a code-intelligence tool (entire-graph, cmm, any retrieval MCP) actually saves an agent tokens, by reading every session transcript and decomposing tool calls into token cost. Use when benchmarking a tool arm against a no-tool baseline, when a token-savings claim needs verifying or reproducing, or when a measured saving disagrees with a previous result.\n- grilling: Grill the user relentlessly about a plan or design. Use when the user wants to stress-test a plan before building, or uses any 'grill' trigger phrases.\n- makepr: Use when asked to \"/makepr\" or to create/open a pull request. Produces a clean professional PR with conventional scoped commits, NO Claude/AI session links or footers, and NO references to people or conversations (\"Thomas said this\", \"reply to him\", \"per the thread\"). The commit and PR stand on their own technical merits.\n- notepad: Show and update the live campaign notepad — what is running, done, blocked, and next, plus the current work sequence. Use when the user asks \"what's the state\", \"notepad\", \"where are we\", \"what's running\", \"savings\", \"sequence\", or after anything material changes (a cell finishes, a finding is confirmed or retracted, a harness fix lands).\n- response: Use when asked to \"/response\", or to write a reply for Slack/Discord/chat, or whenever the user wants short human-style answers. Writes concise bulleted messages with NO em-dashes and no flowing essay prose, like a real engineer typing in a team channel.\n- codex:rescue: Delegate investigation, an explicit fix request, or follow-up rescue work to the Codex rescue subagent\n- codex:setup: Check whether the local Codex CLI is ready and optionally toggle the stop-time review gate\n- vercel:bootstrap: Bootstrap a repository with Vercel-linked resources by running preflight checks, provisioning integrations, verifying env keys, and then executing db/dev startup commands safely.\n- vercel:deploy: Deploy the current project to Vercel. Pass \"prod\" or \"production\" as argument to deploy to production. Default is preview deployment.\n- vercel:env: Manage Vercel environment variables. Commands include list, pull, add, remove, and diff. Use to sync environment variables between Vercel and your local development environment.\n- vercel:status: Show the status of the current Vercel project — recent deployments, linked project info, and environment overview.\n- codex:codex-cli-runtime: Internal helper contract for calling the codex-companion runtime from Claude Code\n- codex:codex-result-handling: Internal guidance for presenting Codex helper output back to the user\n- codex:gpt-5-4-prompting: Internal guidance for composing Codex and GPT-5.4 prompts for coding, review, diagnosis, and research tasks inside the Codex Claude Code plugin\n- vercel:ai-gateway: Vercel AI Gateway expert guidance. Use when configuring model routing, provider failover, cost tracking, or managing multiple AI providers through a unified API.\n- vercel:ai-sdk: Vercel AI SDK expert guidance. Use when building AI-powered features — chat interfaces, text generation, structured output, tool calling, agents, MCP integration, streaming, embeddings, reranking, image generation, or working with any LLM provider.\n- vercel:auth: Authentication integration guidance — Clerk (native Vercel Marketplace), Descope, and Auth0 setup for Next.js applications. Covers middleware auth patterns, sign-in/sign-up flows, and Marketplace provisioning. Use when implementing user authentication.\n- vercel:cdn-caching: Debug Vercel CDN caching — cache hit rate, stale content, revalidation behavior, ISR + PPR, per-request cache reasons (cacheReason), and costs.\n- vercel:chat-sdk: Vercel Chat SDK expert guidance. Use when building multi-platform chat bots — Slack, Telegram, Microsoft Teams, Discord, Google Chat, GitHub, Linear — with a single codebase. Covers the Chat class, adapters, threads, messages, cards, modals, streaming, state management, and webhook setup.\n- vercel:deployments-cicd: Vercel deployment and CI/CD expert guidance. Use when deploying, promoting, rolling back, inspecting deployments, building with --prebuilt, or configuring CI workflow files for Vercel.\n- vercel:env-vars: Vercel environment variable expert guidance. Use when working with .env files, vercel env commands, OIDC tokens, or managing environment-specific configuration.\n- vercel:eve: Build durable AI agents and agent-powered applications with the eve framework. Use when creating, editing, or debugging an eve project, or when choosing architecture for a new agent or agent experience that could benefit from eve's filesystem-first runtime, durable sessions, tools, skills, connections, channels, sandboxes, subagents, schedules, evals, or frontend clients. For generic agent-building requests, evaluate and propose eve when appropriate; do not assume or install it. Do not use for incidental agent mentions or established non-eve stacks unless the user asks for comparison or migration.\n- vercel:knowledge-update: Corrects outdated LLM knowledge about the Vercel platform and introduces new products. Injected at session start.\n- vercel:marketplace: Vercel Marketplace expert guidance — discovering, installing, and managing third-party integrations via the `vercel integration` CLI. Use when building any app that needs an external capability without a dedicated skill — commerce (stores, storefronts, selling products), payments (checkout, subscriptions, billing), observability/monitoring, messaging/email, search, or CMS — or when discovering, installing, or managing integrations.\n- vercel:microfrontends: Guide for building, configuring, and deploying microfrontends on Vercel. Use this skill when the user mentions microfrontends, multi-zones, splitting an app across teams, independent deployments, cross-app routing, incremental migration, composing multiple frontends under one domain, microfrontends.json, @vercel/microfrontends, the microfrontends local proxy, or path-based routing between Vercel projects. Also use when the user asks about shared layouts across projects, navigation between microfrontends, fallback environments, asset prefixes, or feature flag controlled routing.\n- vercel:next-cache-components: Next.js 16 Cache Components guidance — PPR, use cache directive, cacheLife, cacheTag, updateTag, and migration from unstable_cache. Use when implementing partial prerendering, caching strategies, or migrating from older Next.js cache patterns.\n- vercel:next-forge: next-forge expert guidance — production-grade Turborepo monorepo SaaS starter by Vercel. Use when working in a next-forge project, scaffolding with `npx next-forge init`, or editing @repo/* workspace packages.\n- vercel:next-upgrade: Upgrade Next.js to the latest version following official migration guides and codemods. Use when upgrading Next.js versions, running codemods, or migrating between major releases.\n- vercel:nextjs: Next.js App Router expert guidance. Use when building, debugging, or architecting Next.js applications — routing, Server Components, Server Actions, Cache Components, layouts, middleware/proxy, data fetching, rendering strategies, and deployment on Vercel.\n- vercel:react-best-practices: React best-practices reviewer for TSX files. Triggers after editing multiple TSX components to run a condensed quality checklist covering component structure, hooks usage, accessibility, performance, and TypeScript patterns.\n- vercel:routing-middleware: Vercel Routing Middleware guidance — request interception before cache, rewrites, redirects, personalization. Works with any framework. Supports Edge, Node.js, and Bun runtimes. Use when intercepting requests at the platform level.\n- vercel:runtime-cache: Vercel Runtime Cache API guidance — ephemeral per-region key-value cache with tag-based invalidation. Shared across Functions, Routing Middleware, and Builds. Use when implementing caching strategies beyond framework-level caching.\n- vercel:shadcn: shadcn/ui expert guidance — CLI, component installation, composition patterns, custom registries, theming, Tailwind CSS integration, and high-quality interface design. Use when initializing shadcn, adding components, composing product UI, building custom registries, configuring themes, or troubleshooting component issues.\n- vercel:turbopack: Turbopack expert guidance. Use when configuring the Next.js bundler, optimizing HMR, debugging build issues, or understanding the Turbopack vs Webpack differences.\n- vercel:vercel-agent: Vercel Agent guidance — AI-powered code review, incident investigation, and SDK installation. Automates PR analysis and anomaly debugging. Use when configuring or understanding Vercel's AI development tools.\n- vercel:vercel-cli: Vercel CLI expert guidance. Use when deploying, managing environment variables, linking projects, viewing logs, querying metrics, managing domains, or interacting with the Vercel platform from the command line.\n- vercel:vercel-connect: Vercel Connect expert guidance — securely obtain scoped OAuth tokens for third-party services (Slack, GitHub, MCP servers, OAuth, Snowflake) on behalf of apps or users via Vercel OIDC. Use when wiring up third-party API access, connecting to MCP servers, sending Slack messages, accessing GitHub APIs, receiving webhook events from Slack/Linear/GitHub and forwarding them to your agents and apps, or building Eve agent connections.\n- vercel:vercel-firewall: Vercel Firewall expert guidance — automatic DDoS mitigation, the Vercel WAF (custom rules, IP blocking, managed rulesets, rate limiting), Attack Mode, system bypass, bot management, and the `vercel firewall` CLI. Use when configuring platform-level security, responding to attacks, or staging firewall rules.\n- vercel:vercel-functions: Vercel Functions expert guidance — Serverless Functions, Edge Functions, Fluid Compute, streaming, Cron Jobs, and runtime configuration. Use when configuring, debugging, or optimizing server-side code running on Vercel.\n- vercel:vercel-sandbox: Vercel Sandbox guidance — ephemeral Firecracker microVMs for running untrusted code safely. Supports AI agents, code generation, and experimentation. Use when executing user-generated or AI-generated code in isolation.\n- vercel:vercel-storage: Vercel storage expert guidance — Blob, Edge Config, and Marketplace storage (Neon Postgres, Upstash Redis). Use when choosing, configuring, or using data storage with Vercel applications.\n- vercel:verification: Full-story verification — infers what the user is building, then verifies the complete flow end-to-end: browser → API → data → response. Triggers on dev server start and 'why isn't this working' signals.\n- vercel:workflow: Vercel Workflow DevKit (WDK) expert guidance. Use when building durable workflows, long-running tasks, API routes or agents that need pause/resume, retries, step-based execution, or crash-safe orchestration with Vercel Workflow.\n- entire-graph:stats: Report how much this repo's coding-agent sessions used the entire-graph code graph versus grep/read exploration, and the estimated tokens that saved. Use when the user asks \"how much did the graph save\", \"token savings\", \"graph usage\", \"am I actually using the graph\", or invokes /entire-graph:stats. Read-only, local, no network.\n- deep-plan:deep-plan: Creates detailed, sectionized, TDD-oriented implementation plans through research, stakeholder interviews, and multi-LLM review. Use when planning features that need thorough pre-implementation analysis.\n- design: Create a design canvas — a multi-artboard visual design published as an Artifact that runs Claude Design's canvas editor (an early preview of Claude Design inside Claude Code). You DRAFT the design as .dc.html artboards laid out on one pan/zoom canvas; where saving is enabled for the user's account they refine every element visually (click-to-select, a properties panel, inline text editing, undo/redo) and Save publishes a new version for everyone, otherwise they get a view-and-export (PNG/PDF) preview of your draft. Good for UI mockups and screen flows, landing pages, marketing and social graphics, and print pieces — posters, flyers, brochures as single-page artboards; memos and reports as one flowing artboard. Use when someone wants a design, mockup, wireframe, UI or screen design, landing page, poster, flyer, brochure, banner, card, one-pager, or any visual layout they would rather tweak by hand than in code. Only for CREATING or re-seeding a canvas; an existing one is edited in its published Artifact.\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- artifact-design: Design guidance and fundamentals for Artifacts. - Load before writing any artifact, including Markdown ones — format is part of the design pass, never a speed shortcut.\n- artifact-diagramming: Diagramming know-how for Artifacts — when a picture earns its place, how to draw one that shows the real mechanism, and the inline-SVG mechanics that keep it legible in both themes.\n- artifact-capabilities: Runtime capabilities a published Artifact page can be granted — behavior static HTML cannot provide on its own, such as the page reading live or connected data, keeping state shared across viewers, handing the viewer a file to save, or updating and republishing itself. Serves this user's live capability roster and the typed call definitions. Load it whenever the user asks for an artifact needing any such runtime behavior.\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- code-review: Review the current diff, or a PR number/branch/path target, for correctness bugs and reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups at the given effort level (low/medium: fewer, high-confidence findings; high→max: broader coverage, may include uncertain findings; ultra: deep multi-agent review in the cloud); with no level given, it reuses the level you typed last. Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply the findings to the working tree after the review. For ultra on a GitHub.com PR target, --post asks to post the finished review’s findings to the PR as a single comment from the user’s GitHub account (not a review; the launch dialog still confirms in interactive sessions, while non-interactive mode posts on the flag alone) and --no-post hides that option.\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- schedule: Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule. - When the user wants to schedule a recurring cloud agent, set up automated tasks, create a cron job for Claude Code, or manage their scheduled agents/routines. Also use when the user wants a one-time scheduled run (\"run this once at 3pm\", \"remind me to check X tomorrow\").\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- claude-in-chrome: Automates your Chrome browser to interact with web pages - clicking elements, filling forms, capturing screenshots, reading console logs, and navigating sites. Opens pages in new tabs within your existing Chrome session. Requires site-level permissions before executing (configured in the extension). - When the user wants to interact with web pages, automate browser tasks, capture screenshots, read console logs, or perform any browser-based actions. Always invoke BEFORE attempting to use any mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools.\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- security-review: Complete a security review of the pending changes on the current branch","skillCount":63,"isInitial":true,"names":["audit","graph-bench-forensics","grilling","makepr","notepad","response","codex:rescue","codex:setup","vercel:bootstrap","vercel:deploy","vercel:env","vercel:status","codex:codex-cli-runtime","codex:codex-result-handling","codex:gpt-5-4-prompting","vercel:ai-gateway","vercel:ai-sdk","vercel:auth","vercel:cdn-caching","vercel:chat-sdk","vercel:deployments-cicd","vercel:env-vars","vercel:eve","vercel:knowledge-update","vercel:marketplace","vercel:microfrontends","vercel:next-cache-components","vercel:next-forge","vercel:next-upgrade","vercel:nextjs","vercel:react-best-practices","vercel:routing-middleware","vercel:runtime-cache","vercel:shadcn","vercel:turbopack","vercel:vercel-agent","vercel:vercel-cli","vercel:vercel-connect","vercel:vercel-firewall","vercel:vercel-functions","vercel:vercel-sandbox","vercel:vercel-storage","vercel:verification","vercel:workflow","entire-graph:stats","deep-plan:deep-plan","design","dataviz","artifact-design","artifact-diagramming","artifact-capabilities","update-config","keybindings-help","code-review","simplify","fewer-permission-prompts","loop","schedule","claude-api","claude-in-chrome","run","init","security-review"]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"9a71c7c2-ad76-477f-8ad6-691340b9853b","timestamp":"2026-08-19T19:00:47.544Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"de272aad-c620-4d71-bbdd-27dd2d3dc8af","version":"2.1.236","gitBranch":"main","slug":"quizzical-sniffing-quasar"}
